const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 01 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 01 — Ten Pages: Bennett Coleman, the Mass Media and Who Owns Them"));
c.push(H2("Covers: What mass media are — press, films, radio, television, and the internet · Ownership patterns — press (private and public), films (private), radio and television (public, then mixed) · Visual and non-visual media and their impact on people's minds · The newsprint case as the doorway to the subject"));
c.push(STORY("New Delhi, 1972. The Times of India, the Hindustan Times, the Indian Express and the Hindu were the largest newspapers in the country, and every sheet of newsprint they printed on was imported under licence. The Government's Newsprint Policy for 1972-73 did four things: no newspaper could have more than ten pages; no common-ownership unit could start a new paper or a new edition; newsprint could not be moved between papers of the same owner; and a paper below ten pages could grow by only twenty per cent. The stated reason was scarcity of newsprint and the wish to help small papers. The big papers, led by Bennett Coleman and Company, said the real effect was to shrink their circulation, their advertising and their editorial columns — a restriction on what they could say and to whom. The Government answered that newsprint was a commodity and its allocation an economic matter, and that the press had no fundamental right to more paper; if free speech was hit at all, the hit was incidental. The Supreme Court, by four to one, struck the policy down. Justice Ray for the majority laid down the test that has governed Indian media law since: the Court looks at the direct effect of a law on the freedom, not at its subject or its form; a restriction on the pages a paper may print is a restriction on its circulation, and circulation is speech; the Government may ration newsprint but it may not use rationing to tell newspapers how big to be. The press, the Court said, is not just an industry that the State regulates like steel — its product is the citizen's information. The case tells you why the ownership of media matters: whoever controls the paper, the spectrum, the screen or the server controls the speech that flows through it."));
c.push(H3("What mass media are"));
c.push(P("**Mass media** are the means by which information, opinion and entertainment reach a large, dispersed audience at the same time through an organised channel — as distinct from personal communication (a letter, a phone call, a public speech). The classical forms, in the order of their arrival, are: the **press** — newspapers, periodicals, books, pamphlets — from the 1780s in India (Hicky's Bengal Gazette, 1780); **films** — from 1913 (Raja Harishchandra) and the Cinematograph Act 1918; **radio** — the Indian Broadcasting Company (1927), All India Radio (1936); **television** — Doordarshan's experimental service (1959), regular service (1965), colour and satellite expansion (1982 Asian Games), private satellite channels (1991-92); and the **internet and digital media** — public internet (1995), web news, social media, OTT streaming and podcasts, now regulated under the Information Technology Act 2000 and its 2021 Rules. The law treats each medium differently because each differs in reach, immediacy, cost of entry, and the audience's ability to choose and to reflect — the difference the syllabus calls **visual and non-visual media**."));
c.push(H3("Ownership patterns — the press"));
c.push(P("The Indian press is **privately owned**. Ownership takes four forms identified by the Press Commissions: **individual and family proprietorship** (the Jains of the Times of India, the Goenkas of the Express, the Birlas of the Hindustan Times, the Kasturis of the Hindu, the Sarkars of the Ananda Bazar); **joint-stock companies**; **trusts and societies** (the Tribune Trust; the Hindu's Kasturi and Sons); and **co-operatives** and **political-party papers** (once significant — the National Herald, Janashakti). Public ownership of the press is negligible: the Government publishes 'Yojana', 'Kurukshetra', 'Employment News' and the Gazette, and the Press Information Bureau and the Directorate of Advertising and Visual Publicity (now the Central Bureau of Communication) manage Government communication and advertising — the latter being the State's main lever over the press, since Government advertising is a large share of newspaper revenue (Common Cause v. Union of India, 2016 — guidelines on Government advertising). The **Registrar of Newspapers for India** (under the Press and Registration of Books Act 1867, replaced by the Press and Registration of Periodicals Act 2023 from 1 March 2024) registers titles; there are about 1.5 lakh registered publications, of which some 20,000 are dailies. **Concentration**: the First Press Commission (1954) and the Second (1982) both worried about 'common ownership units', 'chain' and 'multiple' ownership, and cross-media ownership; the recommended Newspaper Development Commission and diffusion of ownership were never implemented, and the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Act 1969 was never seriously applied to the press (Episode 04). **Foreign ownership**: barred since 1955 (the Cabinet decision after the First Press Commission), relaxed in 2002 to 26 per cent for news and current affairs and 74 per cent (now 100 per cent under the automatic route for non-news) for other publications; 26 per cent remains the cap for news print media and for FM radio and news television uplinking."));
c.push(H3("Ownership patterns — films"));
c.push(P("Films are **privately produced**: producers, studios and, from the 1990s, corporate houses (film financing was recognised as an 'industry' in 1998, opening bank finance); distribution and exhibition are private, in single screens and multiplexes; satellite, OTT and music rights are sold to private broadcasters and platforms. The State's role is the certifier (the Central Board of Film Certification under the Cinematograph Act 1952 — Episode 05), the financier of a few (the National Film Development Corporation, 1980, from the Film Finance Corporation of 1960), the producer of documentaries (the Films Division, 1948, merged into NFDC in 2022), the archivist (the National Film Archive, Pune), the trainer (the Film and Television Institute of India), and the taxing authority (entertainment tax by States, now GST). Regional industries — Telugu (the largest by output, in Hyderabad after the shift from Madras in the 1990s), Tamil, Hindi, Malayalam, Kannada, Bengali — are private, and the Telugu industry's ownership by a few production families is the standard illustration of concentration in film."));
c.push(H3("Ownership patterns — radio and television"));
c.push(P("Broadcasting was a **Government monopoly** from 1930 (when the Government took over the bankrupt Indian Broadcasting Company) to the 1990s: All India Radio and Doordarshan were departments of the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting, staffed by civil servants, funded from the Consolidated Fund and advertising, and legally protected by Sec 4 of the Indian Telegraph Act 1885, which gives the Central Government the exclusive privilege of establishing, maintaining and working telegraphs (which include wireless and broadcasting). The **Prasar Bharati Act 1990** (in force from 1997) converted AIR and DD into an autonomous corporation, the Prasar Bharati (Broadcasting Corporation of India) — Episode 06. **Private ownership** entered by the back door: satellite television beamed from abroad (Star, Zee, 1991-92) received through cable operators, legitimised by the Cable Television Networks (Regulation) Act 1995; and by the front door after the Supreme Court held in the Cricket Association of Bengal case (1995) that the airwaves are public property that the Government does not own but holds in trust — leading to private uplinking of satellite channels (guidelines 2000, 2005, 2022), private FM radio (Phase I 2000, Phase II 2005, Phase III 2011, e-auctions), community radio (2002, 2006), direct-to-home (2003), and now over 900 private satellite channels, about 400 private FM stations and 500 community radio stations. **Public** broadcasting remains with Prasar Bharati (AIR's 470 stations, DD's national, regional and DD Free Dish services). **Private news** on radio is still barred (private FM may only relay AIR news), and news television needs an uplinking permission with Indian ownership and control. The mixed pattern is: public monopoly in terrestrial broadcasting, private plurality in satellite and cable, and public and private co-existing in FM."));
c.push(H3("Visual and non-visual media — the difference and the impact"));
c.push(P("The **non-visual (print) media** — newspapers, periodicals, books — reach a literate reader who chooses what to read, reads at his own pace, can stop and reflect, re-read and compare; the message is received through the intellect; the reader pays for the paper and can put it down. **Radio** is auditory and non-visual too, but reaches the illiterate, is immediate and intimate, and its impact is in the voice. The **visual media** — films, television, video, and now short-video social media — combine sight and sound, moving image and music, in a darkened hall or a lit living room, before a mixed audience including children, and reach the emotions before the intellect; the viewer does not choose the pace, cannot 'skip' a scene as he skips a paragraph, and receives the message as a whole. The courts have built the law on this difference: **K.A. Abbas v. Union of India (1970)** — 'the motion picture is able to stir up emotions more deeply than any other product of art... a person reading a book or other writing or hearing a speech or viewing a painting or sculpture is not so deeply stirred as by seeing a motion picture' — which is why pre-censorship of films is valid though pre-censorship of the press is not (Episode 05); **S. Rangarajan v. P. Jagjivan Ram (1989)** — the same point, but the remedy for a film that offends is not a ban but the same free-speech answer; **Odyssey Communications v. Lokvidayan Sanghatana (1988)** and **Ramesh v. Union of India (1988)** — television's reach into every home makes the 'reasonable, strong-minded, firm and courageous' viewer the test, not the hypersensitive one. The **impact on people's minds** the syllabus asks about is the sociological literature on media effects — agenda-setting (the media tell us what to think about), cultivation (heavy television viewers see the world as television shows it), the 'hypodermic needle' and its refutation, imitation and desensitisation to violence, the influence of advertising on children (the Cable Television Advertising Code bars ads that exploit children), 'trial by media' and the presumption of innocence (Sahara v. SEBI, 2012), the spread of rumour and hate through mobile video (the 2018 lynching incidents and the Supreme Court's directions in Tehseen Poonawalla), and the 'filter bubble' of social media algorithms — and the law's response to each, which the later episodes take up."));
c.push(TBL([
["Medium","Ownership","Governing law","Regulator"],
["Press","Private (families, companies, trusts); negligible public","PRB Act 1867 → Press and Registration of Periodicals Act 2023; Working Journalists Act 1955 (now OSH Code 2020); Press Council Act 1978","Press Registrar General; Press Council of India (self-regulatory, censure only)"],
["Films","Private; State certifies, finances a few, taxes","Cinematograph Act 1952 (amended 2023); Cine-Workers Acts; Copyright Act 1957","CBFC; (FCAT abolished 2021 — appeals to High Court)"],
["Radio","Public (AIR/Prasar Bharati); private FM (non-news); community radio","Indian Telegraph Act 1885 Sec 4 → Telecommunications Act 2023; Prasar Bharati Act 1990; FM policy phases; Indian Wireless Telegraphy Act 1933","Ministry of I&B; WPC wing; TRAI (tariffs, interconnection)"],
["Television","Public (DD); private satellite channels (uplinking/downlinking guidelines); cable and DTH private","Prasar Bharati Act; Cable Television Networks (Regulation) Act 1995 and Rules 1994 (Programme and Advertising Codes); TRAI Act 1997 (broadcasting notified 2004)","Ministry of I&B; TRAI; NBDSA and BCCC (self-regulation); Inter-Departmental Committee"],
["Internet / digital","Private platforms, mostly foreign","IT Act 2000 (Secs 66A struck, 69A, 79); IT (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules 2021; DPDP Act 2023","MeitY; Ministry of I&B (digital news, OTT — three-tier); Grievance Appellate Committees"]
],[1300,2500,3200,2000]));
c.push(CASE("Bennett Coleman and Co v. Union of India (1972) 2 SCC 788","The Newsprint Policy's ten-page ceiling and bar on new editions directly restricted circulation and content and violated Art 19(1)(a); the test is the direct effect of the law on the freedom, not its subject matter (the 'pith and substance' test does not apply to fundamental rights); a newspaper's freedom includes the freedom to decide its size, circulation and content; Justice Mathew's dissent argued for the Government's power to promote diffusion of ownership and small papers — the two views frame the ownership debate."));
c.push(CASE("Secretary, Ministry of I&B v. Cricket Association of Bengal (1995) 2 SCC 161","Airwaves are public property to be used for the public good; a Government monopoly of broadcasting is not justified by Art 19(2); the right to impart and receive information includes the right to telecast, subject to regulation by an independent public authority."));
c.push(CASE("K.A. Abbas v. Union of India (1970) 2 SCC 780","Films are a form of speech under Art 19(1)(a) but their greater impact justifies pre-censorship, provided the standards are definite and the procedure is fair."));
c.push(DIG("Ownership today is measured by the Ministry of I&B's cross-media ownership studies and the TRAI's 2014 recommendations (never implemented) on limits across print, television and radio in a market; the Competition Commission examines media mergers (Reliance-Disney 2024) under the Competition Act 2002; and the 2023 Press and Registration of Periodicals Act moved title registration online through the Press Sewa portal."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Mass media', 'Visual and non-visual media', 'Ownership of the press in India', 'Bennett Coleman case', 'Prasar Bharati', 'Common ownership unit'. Part B: 'Describe the types of mass media and the patterns of ownership of the press, films, radio and television in India' — the table, with the Press Commissions on concentration and the CAB case on the airwaves; or 'Distinguish visual from non-visual media and explain how the difference is reflected in the law' — Abbas, Rangarajan, Ramesh, the pre-censorship point. Part C: the Government limits a newspaper's pages to save newsprint — Bennett Coleman, direct effect on circulation, void; a State bans private FM stations from broadcasting news — permissible under the current policy but challengeable after CAB; a newspaper owner starts a television channel and an FM station in the same city — no Indian cross-media law prohibits it (TRAI's recommendations were not adopted)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 02 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 02 — The Price of a Page: Sakal Papers and the Freedom of the Press under Article 19(1)(a)"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Freedom of speech and expression under Art 19(1)(a) and whether it includes the press · The reasonable restrictions of Art 19(2) · The four newspaper cases — Express Newspapers, Sakal, Bennett Coleman, Indian Express · Price and Page Schedule Regulation · Newsprint Control Order · Pre-censorship of the press — Brij Bhushan and Romesh Thappar · Advertisement and Art 19(1)(a) — Hamdard Dawakhana and Tata Press"));
c.push(STORY("Poona, 1960. Sakal, the Marathi daily founded by N.B. Parulekar, sold for seven paise on weekdays and had built a circulation on giving readers more pages for the price than any competitor. The Daily Newspapers (Price and Page) Act 1956 and the Price and Page Order 1960, made on the recommendation of the First Press Commission, fixed the minimum price a newspaper had to charge according to the number of pages it printed, and limited the space that could be given to advertisements. The idea was to stop the big papers from selling below cost, subsidised by advertising, and crushing the small ones. Sakal said the Order forced it either to raise its price (and lose readers) or cut its pages (and lose what it could say). The Government answered that this was economic regulation of a trade under Art 19(6) — a newspaper is a business, and the Order regulated its business. The Supreme Court disagreed. Justice Mudholkar held that the freedom of speech and expression includes the freedom of the press, that the freedom includes the volume of what is published and the circulation it reaches, and that a law that restricts the freedom of speech cannot be saved by saying that it is meant to regulate a business, because Art 19(2) — not Art 19(6) — is the only source of permissible restrictions on speech, and none of its grounds (security, public order, decency, defamation, contempt, incitement, the sovereignty of India, friendly relations) covers the protection of small papers from big ones. The State, the Court said, cannot curtail one man's freedom to advance another's. The case fixes the two propositions every answer on press freedom needs: the press has no separate right but is fully within Art 19(1)(a); and the only restrictions are those of Art 19(2), read strictly."));
c.push(H3("Art 19(1)(a) and the press"));
c.push(P("**Art 19(1)(a)**: all citizens shall have the right to freedom of speech and expression. Unlike the First Amendment of the United States ('Congress shall make no law... abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press'), the Indian Constitution does not mention the press. Dr Ambedkar told the Constituent Assembly it was unnecessary: the press is only the citizen exercising his freedom through a printing press; the editor and the proprietor are citizens with the same right as any other, and no more. The Supreme Court has consistently held that **freedom of the press is included in Art 19(1)(a)** (Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras, 1950; Brij Bhushan v. State of Delhi, 1950; Express Newspapers, 1958; Sakal, 1962; Bennett Coleman, 1972; Indian Express, 1985), and that it comprises: (1) the freedom to **publish** — to print what the publisher chooses, without prior restraint; (2) the freedom of **circulation** — to reach whom he chooses (Romesh Thappar: 'liberty of circulation is as essential to that freedom as the liberty of publication; indeed, without circulation the publication would be of little value'); (3) the freedom to **choose content, size and volume** (Sakal; Bennett Coleman); (4) the freedom **from a burden singling out the press** — a tax or licence that falls on the press as such or is excessive (Indian Express — customs duty on newsprint must be tested for its effect on the press); (5) the right of the reader to **receive** information (Indian Express; Reliance Petrochemicals v. Indian Express, 1988; Secretary, I&B v. CAB, 1995 — the right to information and to be informed is part of Art 19(1)(a)); (6) the **right to know** what the Government does (Raj Narain; S.P. Gupta — Episode 08); and (7) the freedom against **pre-censorship** (Brij Bhushan — the Organiser's compulsory pre-scrutiny order struck down; the press cannot be made to submit copy before publication except in an emergency). Limits inherent in the definition: the right is that of citizens (a company's shareholders and editors may sue — Bennett Coleman; a foreign company cannot); it is not a right to Government advertising, newsprint, or a licence; and it does not include a right of access to Government information beyond the RTI Act or a right to enter premises."));
c.push(H3("Reasonable restrictions — Art 19(2)"));
c.push(P("**Art 19(2)** (as amended by the First Amendment 1951 and the Sixteenth 1963): nothing in Art 19(1)(a) prevents the State from making any law imposing **reasonable restrictions** in the interests of the **sovereignty and integrity of India**, the **security of the State**, **friendly relations with foreign States**, **public order**, **decency or morality**, or in relation to **contempt of court**, **defamation** or **incitement to an offence**. The eight grounds are exhaustive — a restriction not traceable to one of them fails (Sakal; Shreya Singhal v. Union of India, 2015 — Sec 66A of the IT Act struck down because 'annoyance', 'inconvenience' and 'grossly offensive' fit no ground). 'In the interests of' is wider than 'for the maintenance of' but the restriction must have a **proximate** connection with the ground (Superintendent, Central Prison v. Ram Manohar Lohia, 1960 — 'public order' requires a real and proximate nexus, not a remote or fanciful one; Kedar Nath Singh, 1962 — sedition limited to incitement to violence). The restriction must be **reasonable** — a law, not executive fiat; not excessive or arbitrary; with procedural safeguards; judged by the direct effect on the right (Bennett Coleman) and, since Modern Dental College (2016) and Puttaswamy (2017), by proportionality — legitimate aim, suitability, necessity and balance. The First Amendment was passed because Romesh Thappar (a ban on Cross Roads' entry into Madras under the Madras Maintenance of Public Order Act, struck down since 'public safety' was wider than 'security of the State') and Brij Bhushan had left the State without a 'public order' ground; the Amendment added 'public order', 'friendly relations with foreign States' and 'incitement to an offence', and inserted 'reasonable', so that the restrictions are reviewable."));
c.push(H3("The four newspaper cases"));
c.push(P("**(1) Express Newspapers (Private) Ltd v. Union of India (1958)** — the Working Journalists (Conditions of Service) and Miscellaneous Provisions Act 1955 and the Wage Board's decision were challenged as burdening the press; the Court upheld the Act: a law regulating the conditions of service of newspaper employees, applying to the press as an industry like any other, does not violate Art 19(1)(a) unless its direct and inevitable effect is to restrict circulation or content; but the Wage Board's decision was struck down for taking into account the capacity of the industry as a whole rather than of each unit. The case gave the **'direct and inevitable effect'** test and the proposition that the press cannot claim immunity from general laws — taxation, labour law, industrial law. **(2) Sakal Papers (P) Ltd v. Union of India (1962)** — the Price and Page Order struck down: Art 19(2), not 19(6), governs; the State cannot curtail the freedom of one to protect another; a law restricting page volume and advertisement space restricts speech. **(3) Bennett Coleman and Co v. Union of India (1972)** — the Newsprint Policy's page limit struck down; the direct-effect test; the press's freedom includes its size; the majority rejected the argument that newsprint is a commodity and its allocation an economic measure outside Art 19. **(4) Indian Express Newspapers (Bombay) v. Union of India (1985)** — customs duty on imported newsprint (which had been exempt since 1966) was challenged; the Court held that the press is not immune from taxation, but a tax on the press must be tested by its effect on the freedom — it cannot be so excessive as to cripple; the Government must consider the burden on the press when fixing the duty, and the levy was remitted for reconsideration; the Court also recognised the reader's right to receive and the press's role in a democracy ('the purpose of the press is to advance the public interest by publishing facts and opinions without which a democratic electorate cannot make responsible judgments'). To these add **Romesh Thappar** and **Brij Bhushan** (1950) on circulation and pre-censorship, **Printers (Mysore) v. Assistant Commercial Tax Officer (1994)** (the press is not immune from sales tax on newsprint, but exemption of newspapers from sales tax under Art 286 and Entry 92 reflects their special position), and **Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India (2020)** (the freedom of the press extends to the internet; the Kashmir Times editor's challenge to the internet shutdown — Episode 12)."));
c.push(H3("Price and Page Schedule Regulation"));
c.push(P("The **Daily Newspapers (Price and Page) Act 1956** empowered the Central Government to regulate the prices of daily newspapers in relation to their pages and sizes and the space for advertisements, to prevent unfair competition; the **Daily Newspapers (Price and Page) Order 1960** fixed a price-page schedule (a paper of a given number of pages had to charge at least a given price) and a ceiling on advertisement space. Struck down in **Sakal (1962)**: (a) the Order's direct effect was to curtail circulation (if the price rose) or content (if pages fell); (b) advertising is the economic base of a newspaper — reducing it raises the price and reduces circulation; (c) the object (helping small papers) is not an Art 19(2) ground; (d) the State cannot make a law 'which will unnecessarily restrict the freedom of speech of one person for the benefit of another'. The Act was a dead letter after Sakal and was repealed. The same logic defeated the **newsprint policy's page limit** in Bennett Coleman: the Government sought to distinguish Sakal by saying that the newsprint policy did not fix prices; the Court said the effect was the same."));
c.push(H3("Newsprint and the Newsprint Control Order"));
c.push(P("**Newsprint** is the cheap, uncoated paper newspapers are printed on; India imported almost all of it until the 1980s, and the Government controlled its import (through the State Trading Corporation), its allocation and its price. The **Newsprint Control Order 1962**, made under Sec 3 of the Essential Commodities Act 1955 (newsprint being an essential commodity), required every consumer to obtain an allotment from the Controller and prohibited use beyond it; the annual **Newsprint Policy** fixed the quotas. **Bennett Coleman** held that the Newsprint Control Order itself, as a measure of rationing a scarce commodity, was valid; what was invalid was the use of the allocation policy to dictate pages and editions — the policy went beyond the rationing purpose of the Order and became a restriction on the press. Newsprint was decontrolled in stages (1990s: import under open general licence; 1997: the Newsprint Control Order was repealed; today newsprint is freely importable at a low customs duty — the 2019 Budget's ten per cent duty was cut after protests, echoing Indian Express 1985)."));
c.push(H3("Pre-censorship, licensing and the press"));
c.push(P("India has no licensing of the press: a printing press need only be declared under Sec 4 of the Press and Registration of Books Act 1867 (now registration under the 2023 Act) and a newspaper's title registered with the Press Registrar; no permission is needed to publish. **Pre-censorship** — submitting copy before publication — was imposed only during the 1975 Emergency (the Central Censor under the Defence and Internal Security of India Rules; the Prevention of Publication of Objectionable Matter Act 1976, repealed 1977) and in war (the 1962 and 1971 press advisories). **Brij Bhushan v. State of Delhi (1950)** struck down an order under Sec 7(1)(c) of the East Punjab Public Safety Act requiring the Organiser to submit all communal matter for scrutiny before publication — pre-censorship is a restriction on the freedom, permissible only if it falls under Art 19(2), which it did not. The Court has since said that the general rule is against prior restraint and that post-publication remedies (defamation, contempt, prosecution) are the norm (R. Rajagopal v. State of T.N., 1994 — no prior restraint on the Auto Shankar autobiography; Sahara v. SEBI, 2012 — a 'postponement order' is possible in a pending trial only on a real and substantial risk to a fair trial, as a last resort). The other legal controls on the press are post-publication: the Press Council's censure; the Official Secrets Act 1923; the Contempt of Courts Act 1971; the IPC (defamation, obscenity, sedition, hate speech — Episode 03); the Emblems and Names Act; Sec 144 CrPC (BNSS Sec 163) orders in emergencies; and, since 2009, Sec 69A of the IT Act blocking for online content."));
c.push(H3("Advertisement — is it within freedom of speech and expression?"));
c.push(P("**Hamdard Dawakhana v. Union of India (1960)** — the Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act 1954 was challenged; the Court held that an advertisement is a form of speech, but its true character is determined by its object: an advertisement of a commercial nature, promoting trade and business, is not within the freedom of speech but is a part of the business protected (and restrictable) under Art 19(1)(g) and 19(6); the Act's prohibition of advertisements of magic remedies was valid. This was narrowed in **Tata Press Ltd v. Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Ltd (1995)** — MTNL claimed the exclusive right to publish the telephone directory with its Yellow Pages; Tata Press's competing Yellow Pages were challenged; the Court held that **'commercial speech' is protected under Art 19(1)(a)**: advertising is the dissemination of information about products, the public has a right to receive it, and it is the economic base of the free press; it can be restricted only under Art 19(2) (deceptive, unfair, misleading and untruthful advertising can be regulated), and Hamdard Dawakhana is confined to advertisements that are themselves objectionable (magic remedies) — the wider dictum that commercial advertisement is outside Art 19(1)(a) was disapproved. So: (1) advertisements are speech; (2) commercial speech is within Art 19(1)(a) but at a lower level of protection; (3) restrictions on false, misleading, obscene or harmful advertising are 'reasonable' (the Consumer Protection Act 2019's misleading-advertisement provisions and the CCPA — Indian Medical Association v. Union of India, 2024, the Patanjali contempt; the Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products Act 2003 Sec 5's ban on tobacco advertising; the Cable TV Advertising Code; the Pre-conception and Pre-natal Diagnostic Techniques Act Sec 22's ban on sex-selection advertising; the Emblems and Names Act); (4) Government advertising is a public resource that must be distributed fairly and not used to reward or punish (Common Cause, 2016 — the Supreme Court's guidelines and the Committee on Content Regulation in Government Advertising; the Ushodaya Publications, Sahara v. Prasar Bharati and the earlier Ushodaya v. Government of A.P. cases on withdrawal of Government advertisements as coercion)."));
c.push(CASE("Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras, AIR 1950 SC 124","A ban on the entry and circulation of Cross Roads under a 'public safety' law was struck down; the freedom of the press and of circulation is part of Art 19(1)(a); 'public safety' is wider than 'security of the State' — led to the First Amendment."));
c.push(CASE("Brij Bhushan v. State of Delhi, AIR 1950 SC 129","Pre-censorship of the Organiser struck down; prior restraint is a restriction on the freedom and needs an Art 19(2) ground."));
c.push(CASE("Express Newspapers v. Union of India, AIR 1958 SC 578","The Working Journalists Act upheld; the press is subject to general laws; the test is the direct and inevitable effect on circulation and content; the Wage Board's award struck down on its merits."));
c.push(CASE("Sakal Papers v. Union of India, AIR 1962 SC 305","Price and Page Order struck down; Art 19(2) alone governs restrictions on speech; the State cannot restrict one's freedom to help another; volume and circulation are within the freedom."));
c.push(CASE("Indian Express Newspapers (Bombay) v. Union of India (1985) 1 SCC 641","Customs duty on newsprint must be tested for its effect on the press; excessive taxation is a restriction; the Government must weigh the burden; the reader's right to receive information."));
c.push(CASE("Tata Press v. MTNL (1995) 5 SCC 139","Commercial speech (advertising) is within Art 19(1)(a); the public's right to receive commercial information; Hamdard Dawakhana narrowed."));
c.push(DIG("Online advertising now exceeds print and television advertising in India; the 2021 IT Rules and the CCPA's Guidelines for Prevention of Misleading Advertisements 2022 govern influencers and surrogate advertising; Government advertising on social media is under the Common Cause guidelines; and the Anuradha Bhasin ruling extends the four newspaper cases' principles to the internet — a shutdown that stops an online newspaper's circulation is a restriction on the press."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Freedom of the press', 'Art 19(2) grounds', 'Price and Page Order', 'Newsprint Control Order', 'Pre-censorship of the press', 'Commercial speech', 'Hamdard Dawakhana'. Part B: 'Is freedom of the press a fundamental right in India? Discuss with reference to the newspaper cases' — Ambedkar's view, the seven components, the four cases, Romesh Thappar and Brij Bhushan; or 'Explain the reasonable restrictions on the freedom of the press under Art 19(2) with cases'; or 'Are advertisements within the freedom of speech and expression? Discuss Hamdard Dawakhana and Tata Press'. Part C: a State fixes the minimum price of dailies by page count — Sakal, void; the Centre imposes a heavy customs duty on newsprint — Indian Express, must weigh effect, may be struck if excessive; a magistrate orders a paper to submit its communal reports for scrutiny — Brij Bhushan, void; an ad for a 'guaranteed cure for diabetes' is banned — Hamdard Dawakhana and the 1954 Act, valid; a telecom company bars a private directory — Tata Press, the competitor's commercial speech is protected."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 03 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 03 — The Autobiography of a Killer: Rajagopal, Defamation, Obscenity, Blasphemy and Sedition"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Defamation — civil and criminal (Secs 499-502 IPC; BNS Sec 356), defences, Subramanian Swamy, privacy and public officials (Rajagopal) · Obscenity — Sec 292 IPC (BNS Sec 294), Hicklin to community standards · Blasphemy — Sec 295A (BNS Sec 299) · Sedition — Sec 124A, Kedar Nath, the 2022 suspension and BNS Sec 152 · Hate speech Sec 153A · Contempt of court and the press"));
c.push(STORY("Madras, 1994. Gauri Shankar, known as Auto Shankar, had been convicted of six murders and sentenced to death. In Salem jail, awaiting the gallows, he wrote his autobiography and sent it, through his wife, to the Tamil weekly Nakkheeran, which announced it would publish it in instalments. The book named police officers and prison officials as his partners in crime. The Inspector General of Prisons wrote to the magazine that the autobiography was a forgery, that the prisoner had not written it, that publication would defame officials and violate prison rules, and that if it were published the State would sue. Nakkheeran's editor R. Rajagopal went to the Supreme Court. Justice Jeevan Reddy's judgment did three things. It recognised, for the first time in India, a **right to privacy** implicit in Art 21 — a right to be let alone, so that nobody may publish anything about a person's family, marriage, procreation, motherhood, child-bearing or education without consent, whether true or not, unless the matter is in public records or the person is a public figure acting in public. It held that **public officials have no privacy in their official conduct**, and no action for defamation lies against the press for publication about official conduct unless the publisher acted with reckless disregard for truth — the Indian version of New York Times v. Sullivan. And it held that the State cannot obtain a **prior restraint** against publication; the remedy for defamation is an action after the event. Auto Shankar was hanged in 1995; his book was published; the case is cited whenever the press writes about the powerful."));
c.push(H3("Defamation — civil"));
c.push(P("**Civil defamation** is a tort: the publication of a false statement about a person that lowers him in the estimation of right-thinking members of society, exposes him to hatred, contempt or ridicule, or causes him to be shunned; the remedies are damages and injunction. **Libel** is defamation in permanent form (writing, print, picture, film, broadcast — Sec 1 of the Defamation Act 1952 in England); **slander** is transient (speech, gesture); in India the distinction matters little because the civil law is uncodified common law and Indian courts treat both alike, and the criminal law (Sec 499) makes no distinction. **Elements**: (1) a defamatory statement — judged by the natural and ordinary meaning to a reasonable reader, or by innuendo (a hidden meaning known to those with special knowledge); (2) referring to the plaintiff — identifiable, even if not named (Newstead v. London Express, 1940; a class cannot sue unless small enough that each member is pointed at); (3) published to a third person — every repetition is a fresh publication, and on the internet each download is publication (the 'multiple publication' rule; the Limitation Act Art 75-76: one year from publication). **Defences**: (a) **justification** (truth) — a complete defence in civil law; (b) **fair comment** on a matter of public interest — an honest expression of opinion on true facts; (c) **privilege** — absolute (Parliament and legislatures under Arts 105 and 194; judicial proceedings; communications between spouses; Art 361 for the President and Governors) and qualified (fair and accurate reports of Parliament, courts and public meetings; communications made in the discharge of a duty or the protection of an interest — 'without malice'); (d) **consent**; (e) **apology and offer of amends** (mitigation); (f) **innocent dissemination** (news vendors, libraries, and under Sec 79 of the IT Act, intermediaries). **Damages** in India have been modest (₹100 crore suits are filed but seldom decreed; ₹10 lakh to ₹1 crore is the range in reported decisions — e.g. Bloomberg v. Zee, 2024 — the Supreme Court cautioned against ex parte injunctions in defamation suits; the Bonnard v. Perryman rule: no interim injunction where the defendant pleads justification)."));
c.push(H3("Defamation — criminal (Secs 499-502 IPC; BNS Sec 356)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 499 IPC** (now **Sec 356(1) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023**): whoever, by words either spoken or intended to be read, or by signs or visible representations, makes or publishes any imputation concerning any person, intending to harm, or knowing or having reason to believe that such imputation will harm, the reputation of such person, is said to defame that person. Four Explanations: (1) imputation against a **dead person** if it would harm his reputation if living and is intended to hurt the feelings of his family; (2) against a **company, association or collection of persons**; (3) an imputation in the form of an **alternative or expressed ironically**; (4) 'harm' means lowering the moral or intellectual character, or the character in respect of caste or calling, or the credit, or causing it to be believed that the body is in a loathsome or disgraceful state. **Ten Exceptions** (defences): (1) imputation of truth which the public good requires to be made or published (truth alone is not enough — public good must also be shown); (2) public conduct of public servants — opinion in good faith on the conduct of a public servant in the discharge of his public functions or his character so far as it appears in that conduct; (3) conduct of any person touching any public question; (4) publication of reports of proceedings of courts; (5) merits of a case decided in court, or the conduct of witnesses and others concerned; (6) merits of a public performance (books, plays, films) submitted to the judgment of the public; (7) censure passed in good faith by a person having lawful authority over another (employer, teacher); (8) accusation preferred in good faith to an authorised person (a complaint to the police or a superior); (9) imputation made in good faith for the protection of one's own or another's interest, or for the public good; (10) caution intended for the good of the person to whom it is conveyed. **Sec 500** (BNS 356(2)): punishment — simple imprisonment up to two years, or fine, or both (the BNS adds community service). **Secs 501-502** (BNS 356(3)-(4)): printing or engraving, and selling printed matter, known to be defamatory. **Procedure**: non-cognizable, bailable, compoundable; **Sec 199 CrPC** (BNSS Sec 222) — cognizance only on the complaint of the person aggrieved (with special provisions for defamation of the President, Governors, Ministers and public servants in respect of their public functions — complaint by the Public Prosecutor with the Government's sanction, tried by the Sessions Court); the editor, printer and publisher declared under the PRB Act (Sec 7 presumption) are answerable — an editor is presumed responsible for the selection of matter (K.M. Mathew v. State of Kerala, 1992; but the 'Chief Editor' who is not declared is not: K.M. Mathew v. K.A. Abraham, 2002 — presumption under Sec 7 does not extend beyond the declared editor, though a person actually responsible may still be prosecuted on evidence). **Subramanian Swamy v. Union of India (2016)** — Secs 499-500 upheld against the Art 19(1)(a) challenge: reputation is part of Art 21, criminal defamation is a reasonable restriction under Art 19(2) (which expressly names defamation), the ten exceptions and the requirement of the aggrieved person's complaint are adequate safeguards, and the 'chilling effect' argument was rejected. The Law Commission (267th Report) and the BNS retained the offence."));
c.push(H3("Defamation, privacy and the press — Rajagopal"));
c.push(P("**R. Rajagopal v. State of Tamil Nadu (1994) 6 SCC 632** lays down: (1) the right to privacy is implicit in Art 21 — the 'right to be let alone'; a citizen has a right to safeguard the privacy of his own, his family, marriage, procreation, motherhood, child-bearing and education among other matters, and none can publish anything concerning these without consent, whether truthful or otherwise and whether laudatory or critical — if he does, he violates the right and is liable in damages; (2) the exception: where the matter is based on **public records including court records** — once a matter becomes a matter of public record, the right to privacy no longer subsists and it becomes a legitimate subject for comment by the press — except that in the interests of decency (Art 19(2)) a female victim of sexual assault, kidnap or abduction should not be further subjected to indignity by having her name published; (3) **public officials** — there is no privacy in their acts and conduct relevant to the discharge of official duties, and no action for damages for defamation lies in respect of comment on them, even if the statement is not true, unless the official proves that the publication was made with reckless disregard for truth (the Sullivan standard) — the official's remedy is to show the publisher acted maliciously; (4) the Government, local authorities and other organs of the State have no right to sue for defamation; (5) **no prior restraint**: the State cannot prevent a publication on the ground that it may be defamatory of its officials — the remedy is after publication. The judgment was extended by K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017), which made privacy a fundamental right, and the press must now balance it under the proportionality test (the 2023 Digital Personal Data Protection Act exempts journalistic use only by rule)."));
c.push(H3("Obscenity — Sec 292 IPC (BNS Sec 294)"));
c.push(P("**Sec 292 IPC** (now **BNS Sec 294**): a book, pamphlet, paper, writing, drawing, painting, representation, figure or any other object (the BNS adds 'in electronic form') is deemed obscene if it is **lascivious or appeals to the prurient interest** or if its effect, or (where it comprises two or more distinct items) the effect of any one of its items, is, if taken as a whole, such as to **tend to deprave and corrupt** persons who are likely, having regard to all relevant circumstances, to read, see or hear the matter contained or embodied in it; the offence is to sell, hire, distribute, publicly exhibit, import, export, advertise or profit from obscene material — punishable with two years and ₹2,000 fine on first conviction (BNS: ₹5,000), five years and ₹5,000 (BNS: ₹10,000) on second. **Exceptions**: publication proved to be justified as being for the public good on the ground that it is in the interest of science, literature, art or learning or other objects of general concern; or kept or used bona fide for religious purposes; sculpture, engraving, painting or representation on or in a temple or car used for religious idols; and (Sec 292(2)(a) proviso). **Sec 293** (BNS 295): sale to persons under twenty; **Sec 294** (BNS 296): obscene acts and songs in public. **The tests**: **Ranjit D. Udeshi v. State of Maharashtra (1965)** — Lady Chatterley's Lover; the Court adopted the **Hicklin test** (R. v. Hicklin, 1868: the tendency to deprave and corrupt those whose minds are open to such immoral influences and into whose hands the publication may fall) with the qualification that the work must be judged as a whole, that obscenity must be so preponderant that the work is 'obscene' rather than a work of art with incidental obscenity, and that the public good exception must be considered; Sec 292 was upheld under Art 19(2) 'decency or morality'. **Samaresh Bose v. Amal Mitra (1985)** — Prajapati; the judge must place himself in the position of the author and of the readers of every age group and judge whether the work is likely to deprave, distinguishing vulgarity (which arouses disgust and revulsion) from obscenity (which depraves and corrupts); the novel was not obscene. **Chandrakant Kalyandas Kakodkar v. State of Maharashtra (1970)** — Shama; the standard is that of the contemporary community, not of a hypersensitive reader; a book that describes sex is not obscene unless it is calculated to arouse impure thoughts. **Aveek Sarkar v. State of West Bengal (2014)** — the Boris Becker photograph; the Court discarded Hicklin for the **community standards test** (from Roth v. United States): the material must be judged from the point of view of an average person applying contemporary community standards, in the context in which it appears and the message it conveys; a picture of a nude interracial couple opposing apartheid was not obscene. **Ajay Goswami v. Union of India (2007)** — a newspaper cannot be censored to the level fit for children; the reasonable adult is the standard. **Bobby Art International v. Om Pal Singh Hoon (1996)** — Bandit Queen; nudity and rape depicted to convey the horror of the crime are not obscene. **Director General, Doordarshan v. Anand Patwardhan (2006)** — Father, Son and Holy War: the test is the ordinary person of common sense and prudence, not the hypersensitive. For the internet, **Sec 67 of the IT Act** (obscene material in electronic form — three years and ₹5 lakh, five years and ₹10 lakh for a second conviction), **Sec 67A** (sexually explicit act), **Sec 67B** (child pornography), and the special Act — the **Indecent Representation of Women (Prohibition) Act 1986** (advertisements and publications depicting a woman's figure or form in a manner that is indecent, derogatory or likely to deprave — Sec 3-4, with the same exceptions) — apply."));
c.push(H3("Blasphemy — Sec 295A IPC (BNS Sec 299) and the religious-offences group"));
c.push(P("English law's crime of blasphemy (abolished in 2008) punished vilification of Christianity; India never had a blasphemy law by that name but has the **offences relating to religion** in Chapter XV IPC (BNS Chapter XVI): **Sec 295** (BNS 298) — injuring or defiling a place of worship with intent to insult a religion; **Sec 295A** (BNS 299) — deliberate and malicious acts intended to outrage the religious feelings of any class by insulting its religion or religious beliefs, by words, signs, visible representations or otherwise (the BNS adds 'through electronic means') — three years, cognizable, non-bailable; **Sec 296** (BNS 300) — disturbing a religious assembly; **Sec 297** (BNS 301) — trespassing on burial places; **Sec 298** (BNS 302) — uttering words with deliberate intent to wound religious feelings. Sec 295A was inserted in 1927 after the Rangila Rasul case (a pamphlet on the Prophet; the Lahore High Court had acquitted under Sec 153A because the section required enmity between classes). **Ramji Lal Modi v. State of U.P. (1957)** — Sec 295A upheld under Art 19(2) 'public order' because it punishes only deliberate and malicious insults (the aggravated form of insult calculated to disrupt public order), not every insult; the requirement of malice and deliberateness is the safeguard. **Baragur Ramachandrappa v. State of Karnataka (2007)** — forfeiture under Sec 95 CrPC of a novel on Basaveshwara upheld; the State may forfeit a book that appears to it to be punishable under Sec 295A, and the Court in Sec 96 proceedings examines whether the grounds are stated and are reasonable. **Sec 95-96 CrPC** (BNSS 98-99) — the power of forfeiture of publications punishable under Secs 124A, 153A, 153B, 292, 293 or 295A, with an application to the High Court's special bench of three judges within two months (State of Maharashtra v. Sangharaj Damodar Rupawate, 2010 — the Shivaji book; the grounds must be stated in the notification; the forfeiture was set aside). **Mahendra Singh Dhoni v. Yerraguntla Shyamsundar (2017)** — Sec 295A requires deliberate and malicious intent; a magazine cover depicting the cricketer as Vishnu did not attract it; the complaint was quashed. Films and television are subject to the same sections and to Sec 5B of the Cinematograph Act and the Programme Code."));
c.push(H3("Sedition — Sec 124A IPC and BNS Sec 152"));
c.push(P("**Sec 124A IPC** (inserted 1870; the Wahabi trials, Bal Gangadhar Tilak's prosecutions of 1897 and 1908, Gandhi's of 1922 — 'the prince among the political sections of the IPC designed to suppress the liberty of the citizen'): whoever by words, spoken or written, or by signs or visible representation, brings or attempts to bring into hatred or contempt, or excites or attempts to excite disaffection towards, the Government established by law in India — life imprisonment or three years; the Explanations exclude comments expressing disapprobation of Government measures or actions with a view to obtaining their alteration by lawful means, without exciting hatred, contempt or disaffection. The Constituent Assembly dropped 'sedition' from Art 19(2); **Kedar Nath Singh v. State of Bihar (1962)** upheld the section by reading it down: only those acts that have the intention or tendency to create disorder or disturbance of public order by resort to violence, or incitement to violence, are punishable; strong criticism of the Government is not sedition. The section was misused against journalists (Vinod Dua v. Union of India, 2021 — quashed, every journalist is entitled to Kedar Nath's protection; Siddique Kappan; the Disha Ravi 'toolkit' case), and in **S.G. Vombatkere v. Union of India (2022)** the Supreme Court, pending the Government's reconsideration, directed that all pending trials, appeals and proceedings under Sec 124A be kept in abeyance and that no new FIRs be registered. The **Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023** omitted Sec 124A and enacted **Sec 152** — 'acts endangering sovereignty, unity and integrity of India': whoever, purposely or knowingly, by words, signs, visible representation, electronic communication, financial means or otherwise, excites or attempts to excite secession or armed rebellion or subversive activities, or encourages feelings of separatist activities, or endangers the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India — life imprisonment or seven years; the Explanation preserves comments expressing disapprobation of Government measures with a view to their alteration by lawful means. Sec 152 drops 'disaffection towards the Government' but adds 'subversive activities' and 'separatist feelings', and whether it survives Kedar Nath's incitement test is the open question (the 22nd Law Commission's 279th Report of 2023 had recommended retaining sedition with the incitement requirement). Related: **Sec 153A** (BNS 196) — promoting enmity between groups on grounds of religion, race, place of birth, residence, language, caste — three years (five in a place of worship) — the hate-speech section, requiring two groups and an intention to promote enmity (Bilal Ahmed Kaloo v. State of A.P., 1997; Manzar Sayeed Khan v. State of Maharashtra, 2007 — the author's intention must be gathered from the writing as a whole; Amish Devgan v. Union of India, 2020 — the test of hate speech: the speaker's position, the content, the context, the target group, and whether it incites); **Sec 153B** (BNS 197) — imputations prejudicial to national integration; **Sec 505** (BNS 353) — statements conducing to public mischief, rumours and false news (505(2) — promoting enmity by rumour); and the **Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act** Sec 13 for unlawful activity."));
c.push(H3("Contempt of court and the press"));
c.push(P("**Art 19(2)** lists contempt of court; **Arts 129 and 215** make the Supreme Court and High Courts courts of record with the power to punish contempt; the **Contempt of Courts Act 1971** defines it: **civil contempt** (Sec 2(b)) — wilful disobedience of a court's order or breach of an undertaking; **criminal contempt** (Sec 2(c)) — the publication (by words, spoken or written, signs, visible representation or otherwise) of any matter, or the doing of any act, which (i) scandalises or tends to scandalise, or lowers or tends to lower, the authority of any court; (ii) prejudices or interferes with the due course of any judicial proceeding; (iii) interferes or tends to interfere with the administration of justice. **Defences for the press**: Sec 3 — innocent publication (no reasonable ground to believe a proceeding was pending) and distribution; Sec 4 — fair and accurate report of a judicial proceeding; Sec 5 — **fair criticism of a judicial act** after the case is decided; Sec 6 — complaint against a presiding officer to a superior; Sec 7 — reports of in camera proceedings in limited cases; **Sec 13** (as amended 2006) — no punishment unless the contempt substantially interferes with the due course of justice, and **truth is a valid defence** if in the public interest and bona fide. Sec 12 — six months' simple imprisonment and ₹2,000 fine, with apology as a ground for discharge; Sec 15 — cognizance on the Court's own motion or on the motion of the Attorney General/Advocate General or with his consent; Sec 20 — limitation of one year. The cases: **E.M.S. Namboodiripad v. T.N. Nambiar (1970)** — calling the judiciary 'an instrument of oppression' was contempt; **P.N. Duda v. P. Shiv Shanker (1988)** — a Law Minister's speech criticising the Supreme Court as favouring the rich was not contempt, since it did not impair the administration of justice; the courts should be slow to punish criticism; **Re Arundhati Roy (2002)** — contempt for the affidavit attributing motives; **Re Prashant Bhushan (2020)** — tweets about the Chief Justice and the Court's role held to be contempt, fine of one rupee; the debate on 'scandalising' (abolished in England in 2013; retained in India, the Law Commission's 274th Report of 2018 advising retention of Sec 2(c)(i)); **Sahara India Real Estate v. SEBI (2012)** — the 'postponement order': a court may, on the application of a party, order the postponement of publication of proceedings for a short period where there is a real and substantial risk of prejudice to a fair trial or the proper administration of justice, as a neutralising device — not a prior restraint under Art 19(2) but a temporary deferment, reviewable, applying the necessity and proportionality tests; **M.P. Lohia v. State of West Bengal (2005)** — a news article on a pending dowry-death case was deprecated as interference; the press must not prejudge. **Reporting**: Sec 327 CrPC (BNSS 366) and Sec 228A IPC (BNS 72) bar publication of rape-trial proceedings and the victim's identity; the Juvenile Justice Act Sec 74 and POCSO Sec 23 bar identification of children; the Supreme Court's Swapnil Tripathi (2018) allowed live-streaming; the 2023 press directions require courts to allow reporting of open court proceedings."));
c.push(CASE("R. Rajagopal v. State of Tamil Nadu (1994) 6 SCC 632","Privacy under Art 21; public records exception; no defamation action by public officials for their official conduct unless reckless disregard; the State cannot sue for defamation; no prior restraint."));
c.push(CASE("Subramanian Swamy v. Union of India (2016) 7 SCC 221","Criminal defamation (Secs 499-500) is constitutional; reputation is part of Art 21; Art 19(2) names defamation; the exceptions and complaint requirement are safeguards."));
c.push(CASE("Ranjit D. Udeshi v. State of Maharashtra, AIR 1965 SC 881","Sec 292 upheld; the Hicklin test adopted with the whole-work and public-good qualifications; Lady Chatterley's Lover held obscene."));
c.push(CASE("Aveek Sarkar v. State of West Bengal (2014) 4 SCC 257","Hicklin replaced by the contemporary community standards test; context and message matter; the Becker photograph not obscene."));
c.push(CASE("Ramji Lal Modi v. State of U.P., AIR 1957 SC 620","Sec 295A valid; punishes only deliberate and malicious insults calculated to disrupt public order."));
c.push(CASE("Kedar Nath Singh v. State of Bihar, AIR 1962 SC 955","Sec 124A read down to incitement to violence or tendency to public disorder; criticism of Government is not sedition; S.G. Vombatkere (2022) kept the section in abeyance; BNS Sec 152 replaced it."));
c.push(CASE("Sahara India Real Estate v. SEBI (2012) 10 SCC 603","Postponement orders on media reporting of pending cases as a neutralising device, on a real and substantial risk to fair trial; not prior restraint but temporary and proportionate."));
c.push(CASE("K.M. Mathew v. K.A. Abraham (2002) 6 SCC 670","The Sec 7 PRB Act presumption of responsibility applies only to the declared editor; a chief editor or managing editor may be prosecuted for defamation only on specific allegations of responsibility."));
c.push(DIG("Online defamation is prosecuted under Sec 499/BNS 356 (Sec 66A having been struck down) and sued in the place of download; Sec 79 of the IT Act protects platforms only if they take down on a court or Government order (Shreya Singhal) or, under the 2021 Rules, on a grievance within the timelines; Sec 67 covers online obscenity; the 2021 Rules' Code of Ethics binds digital news publishers to the Press Council norms and the Programme Code; and Sec 69A blocking has been used against sedition-type content — challenged in Twitter v. Union of India (Karnataka HC 2023, upheld)."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Libel and slander', 'Exceptions to Sec 499', 'Hicklin test', 'Community standards test', 'Sec 295A', 'Sedition after Kedar Nath', 'BNS Sec 152', 'Scandalising the court', 'Postponement order', 'Rajagopal case'. Part B: 'Explain the law of defamation as it applies to the press, civil and criminal, with defences and the effect of Rajagopal and Subramanian Swamy'; or 'Trace the law of obscenity from Ranjit Udeshi to Aveek Sarkar'; or 'Discuss sedition — Sec 124A, Kedar Nath, the 2022 order and Sec 152 BNS'; or 'How far does the law of contempt restrict the press? Refer to the 1971 Act and Sahara v. SEBI'. Part C: a paper alleges a Minister took bribes and cannot prove it — Exception 2/3 and Rajagopal: no liability unless reckless disregard, but Sec 199(2) CrPC allows a Public Prosecutor's complaint; a magazine publishes a nude art photograph with an anti-apartheid message — Aveek Sarkar, not obscene; a cartoon depicts a deity in a political satire — Sec 295A needs deliberate and malicious intent (Dhoni case); an editorial calls for the Government to be thrown out by force — Kedar Nath, punishable (now Sec 152); an editorial calls the Government corrupt and incompetent — not sedition; a channel names a rape victim — Sec 228A/BNS 72; a paper publishes a report on a pending murder trial predicting guilt — contempt (Sec 2(c)(ii)), possible postponement order."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 04 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 04 — The Yellow Pages War: Tata Press, Working Journalists, Advertising Revenue and Press Monopolies"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The Working Journalists Act 1955 — who is a working journalist, wages, hours, leave, gratuity, retrenchment, the Wage Boards and the Majithia award, the Labour Codes · Advertisement and the press — revenue, Government advertising, the DAVP/CBC · Press and the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Act — the Press Commissions, concentration, the Competition Act · Press and Registration — the 1867 Act and the 2023 Act · The Press Council of India"));
c.push(STORY("Bombay, 1991. Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited published the telephone directory for Bombay and, with it, the Yellow Pages — classified business listings sold as advertising. Tata Press started its own Tata Press Yellow Pages, a directory of businesses with paid advertisements, and MTNL sued. Under the Indian Telegraph Act and the Telegraph Rules, MTNL argued, only the telephone authority could publish a list of subscribers; the Yellow Pages were part of the directory; and, in any case, advertising was mere commerce and not speech. The Supreme Court gave Tata Press the right to publish. Justice Kuldip Singh held that the Rules covered only the list of subscribers, not a buyers' guide; and then went on to the constitutional question. Advertising, the Court said, is the life-blood of the free press: a newspaper's price covers a fraction of its cost, and advertising pays the rest; cut the advertising and you raise the price and shrink the circulation — the lesson of Sakal. The public has a right to receive commercial information about goods and services; the advertiser has the right to impart it; both are 'commercial speech' within Art 19(1)(a), and the older dictum in Hamdard Dawakhana that advertisement is outside the freedom was confined to advertisements of objectionable things. The case is the anchor for this episode's theme — the press as an economic enterprise, whose employees the law protects, whose revenue the law recognises, and whose concentration the law has tried, feebly, to control."));
c.push(H3("The Working Journalists Act 1955"));
c.push(P("The First Press Commission (1954) found journalists underpaid, insecure and at the mercy of proprietors, and recommended a statute; Parliament passed the **Working Journalists (Conditions of Service) and Miscellaneous Provisions Act 1955**, extended in 1962 to non-journalist newspaper employees and renamed the **Working Journalists and other Newspaper Employees (Conditions of Service) and Miscellaneous Provisions Act 1955**. **Sec 2(f)** — 'working journalist' means a person whose principal avocation is that of a journalist and who is employed as such, either whole-time or part-time, in or in relation to one or more newspaper establishments, and includes an editor, a leader-writer, news editor, sub-editor, feature writer, copy tester, reporter, correspondent, cartoonist, news photographer and proof reader, but does not include a person employed mainly in a managerial or administrative capacity or, being employed in a supervisory capacity, performs mainly managerial functions; **Sec 2(d)** — 'newspaper establishment' (including news agencies); **Sec 2(dd)** — 'non-journalist newspaper employee'. **Sec 3** — the **Industrial Disputes Act 1947** applies to working journalists as if they were workmen (so they get the protections against retrenchment, lay-off, closure and unfair dismissal, and access to labour courts); **Sec 4** — special provisions for **retrenchment** — notice of six months for an editor and three months for others, or wages in lieu; **Sec 5** — **gratuity** — on retirement after three years' service, or on death, or on resignation after three years (the Act's gratuity being more generous than the Payment of Gratuity Act; fifteen days' wages for each completed year); **Sec 6** — **hours of work** — not more than 144 hours in four weeks (36 a week), with a day of rest in seven; **Sec 7** — **leave** — earned leave of one month for eleven months of service, medical leave of one month a year on half pay; **Secs 8-13** — the **Wage Boards** — the Central Government constitutes a Wage Board for working journalists (Sec 9) and one for non-journalists (Sec 13C), of an independent chairman, two employer and two employee representatives, and two independents (Sec 9); it recommends rates of wages after considering the cost of living, the prevalent rates, the circumstances of the newspaper industry in different regions and the capacity to pay; the Central Government by order under **Sec 12** may accept the recommendations with or without modification, and the order is binding — enforced through **Sec 17** (recovery of money due as arrears of land revenue on the Labour Commissioner's certificate) and **Sec 17A** (Working Journalists (Fixation of Rates of Wages) Act 1958 — passed after Express Newspapers struck down the first Wage Board's decision); **Sec 14** — the Act applies to Central Government employees; **Sec 15-16** — inspectors, penalties (fine); **Sec 20** — rules. **The Wage Boards**: the first (Rajadhyaksha, 1956 — struck down in Express Newspapers, 1958, for taking the industry's overall capacity), the Shinde Board (1963), the Palekar Tribunal (1979-80), the Bachawat Board (1989), the Manisana Board (1998-2000) and the **Majithia Board (2007-2010)**, whose recommendations were accepted in 2011; the newspapers' challenge was dismissed in **ABP Pvt Ltd v. Union of India (2014)** — the Act is valid (Express Newspapers followed), the Board's recommendations were not arbitrary, and the Court later monitored compliance through contempt proceedings (2017). **The Labour Codes**: the **Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code 2020** (Sec 134 and the Third Schedule) repeals the Working Journalists Act and the 1958 Act and re-enacts their substance — the definitions of working journalist and newspaper establishment (Sec 2(zzc) and (zt)), hours of work (Sec 25 read with the rules), leave (Sec 32), and the Wage Boards move to the **Code on Wages 2019** (Sec 42 read with the Fifth Schedule? — verify against the notified rules); the Codes were brought into force on 21 November 2025, so the 1955 Act is now the repealed model for the Code's chapter, which the exam still describes by its old name."));
c.push(H3("Advertisement and the press — revenue"));
c.push(P("Newspapers earn 60-70 per cent of revenue from advertising and sell at a cover price far below cost — the 'advertising subsidy' the Sakal and Tata Press judgments recognise. Three consequences: (1) **restrictions on advertising restrict the press** — Sakal (the ad-space ceiling), Tata Press, Bennett Coleman (pages lost were advertising pages); (2) **Government advertising is a lever** — the Directorate of Advertising and Visual Publicity (DAVP, 1955), merged into the **Central Bureau of Communication** (2017), empanels newspapers and channels and releases Central advertisements at fixed rates on circulation criteria; States have their own; **Common Cause v. Union of India (2015-16)** laid down that Government advertising must be for public information, not personal glorification — no photographs of political leaders except the President, Prime Minister and Chief Justice (later extended to Governors and Chief Ministers on review), a Committee on Content Regulation in Government Advertising; and the withdrawal of advertising to punish a newspaper is an unreasonable restriction (Ushodaya Publications v. Government of A.P., 1981 A.P. — Eenadu; the State's ad boycott was held to be a colourable exercise; the Supreme Court in Sahara v. Prasar Bharati and in the Kashmir Times and Dainik Bhaskar disputes has treated advertising allocation as an executive discretion subject to Art 14 and Art 19(1)(a)); (3) **the content of advertising is regulated** — Episode 02's list (the 1954 Act, COTPA, the PCPNDT Act, the Emblems Act, the Indecent Representation Act, the Consumer Protection Act 2019 Secs 2(28) and 89 — misleading advertisement, with the CCPA's power to impose ₹10 lakh, then ₹50 lakh penalties and to ban endorsers, and the Patanjali case; the ASCI's self-regulatory code, applied by the courts as a standard — Bharat Matrimony; the Cable TV Advertising Code; the Press Council's norms on advertisements; 'paid news' — the Press Council's 2010 report, the Election Commission's treatment of paid news as an election expense, and the Ashok Chavan v. Madhavrao Kinhalkar (2014) decision that the Commission may inquire into a candidate's election account); and 'surrogate' and 'advertorial' content which the Consumer Protection Act's 2022 guidelines require to be labelled."));
c.push(H3("Press and the Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Act"));
c.push(P("The **MRTP Act 1969** (in force 1970, repealed by the Competition Act 2002 from 2009) controlled monopolistic, restrictive and unfair trade practices and the expansion of 'dominant undertakings' and large industrial houses (assets above ₹20 crore, later ₹100 crore) through the MRTP Commission and Central Government approval for expansion, mergers and new undertakings (Chapter III, Secs 20-26, deleted in 1991). **Its application to the press**: the Second Press Commission (1982) and the earlier Bhargava Committee recommended that the press be brought under the MRTP Act's monopoly provisions and that a Newspaper Development Commission and Newspaper Registration Authority be set up to check chain and multiple ownership; the newspaper industry resisted, and no Government applied the Act to the press's ownership structure; a few complaints on restrictive trade practices (advertising rates, distribution) went to the MRTP Commission; the Act's unfair-trade-practice provisions (Sec 36A, inserted 1984 — false or misleading advertisements) were applied to advertisers, and this is the Act's real media legacy — the definition of 'unfair trade practice' now in the Consumer Protection Act. The **Competition Act 2002** applies to the media as to any enterprise: anti-competitive agreements (Sec 3 — the Cine Exhibitors and film-producer associations' cartel cases; the FICCI-Multiplex Association case), abuse of dominance (Sec 4 — the Sun TV and Star India complaints; the DTH and cable disputes), and combinations (Sec 5-6 — the Reliance-Disney merger, 2024, approved with conditions on advertising in cricket broadcasts). Cross-media ownership limits recommended by the TRAI (2009, 2014) and the Ministry's consultations have not been enacted. The result: press monopolies are controlled, if at all, by general competition law, and by the market — which the Bennett Coleman majority preferred to State intervention, while Justice Mathew's dissent argued that the diffusion of ownership is itself a value Art 19(1)(a) serves."));
c.push(H3("Registration of the press — 1867 to 2023"));
c.push(P("The **Press and Registration of Books Act 1867** (the oldest media statute in force until 2024) required: every printing press to be declared before the District Magistrate (Sec 4); every newspaper's printer and publisher to make a declaration (Sec 5), with the editor's name printed on every issue (Sec 5(1)) and a presumption that the declared editor is the editor (Sec 7); title verification by the Registrar of Newspapers (Sec 6); delivery of copies to the Government and libraries (Secs 9-11); annual statements (Sec 19D); the Registrar (Sec 19A-19K); penalties for non-declaration (Secs 12-15). The **Press and Registration of Periodicals Act 2023** (in force 1 March 2024) replaced it: registration of periodicals (newspapers and other periodicals of public news) is by an online declaration to the **Press Registrar General** through the Press Sewa portal, with the District Magistrate's role reduced to a sixty-day comment; **Sec 4** bars a person convicted of terrorism or of acts against the security of the State from publishing; **Sec 5** — title allotment and registration; **Sec 6** — the Registrar General's powers to suspend or cancel registration; **Sec 7-8** — appeals to the Press and Registration Appellate Board (the Press Council chairman and two members); **Sec 9** — annual statements; **Sec 10** — the printer need only give intimation; printing presses need no declaration; **Sec 11** — the Registrar's powers of inspection; **Sec 12** — decriminalisation (penalties are now fines, imprisonment only for publishing without registration after a direction to stop, six months); **Sec 15** — books are excluded (the Delivery of Books Act 1954 continues). The presumption of the editor's responsibility (old Sec 7) is not carried into the new Act, which affects defamation prosecutions."));
c.push(H3("The Press Council of India"));
c.push(P("Recommended by the First Press Commission, constituted under the **Press Council Act 1965**, abolished during the Emergency (1976), re-established by the **Press Council Act 1978**: **Sec 4** — a Chairman (by convention a retired Supreme Court judge, nominated by the Speaker, the Rajya Sabha Chairman and a Council member) and 28 members — 13 working journalists (six editors), six from newspaper owners and managers, one from news agencies, three persons of eminence in education, law and literature, and five MPs; three-year term; **Sec 13** — the objects: to preserve the freedom of the press and to maintain and improve the standards of newspapers and news agencies, by helping newspapers maintain independence, building a code of conduct, ensuring the maintenance of high standards of public taste and a sense of responsibility, keeping under review developments likely to restrict the supply of news of public interest, concentration of ownership, and assistance from foreign sources, and promoting a proper functional relationship among all classes of persons in the press; **Sec 14** — the power of **censure**: on a complaint that a newspaper or journalist has offended against the standards of journalistic ethics or public taste, or that an editor or journalist has committed professional misconduct, the Council may, after inquiry, warn, admonish or censure the newspaper, news agency, editor or journalist, or disapprove the conduct, and may direct publication of its findings (Sec 14(1)-(2)); **Sec 15** — powers of a civil court in inquiries (summoning witnesses, documents, evidence on affidavit — but no power to compel disclosure of a journalist's source, Sec 15(2)); the Council's decisions are not subject to appeal (Sec 14(3)) but are reviewable by writ; the Council has **no power to fine, suspend or close** — it is a 'toothless watchdog' (the phrase from the debates), and its **Norms of Journalistic Conduct** (2010, 2019 editions) are its main product — accuracy, right of reply, privacy, not naming victims, caution in communal reporting, no paid news, separation of advertising and news. The Council covers only print (and news agencies); broadcasting has the self-regulatory NBDSA (News Broadcasters and Digital Association's authority) and BCCC (for general entertainment channels) and the statutory Programme Code; the 2021 IT Rules gave digital news publishers a three-tier self-regulatory scheme with the Press Council norms as the Code of Ethics. Proposals for a **Media Council** covering all media (the 2012 Justice Katju controversy; the 2019-23 Broadcasting Bill drafts) have not passed."));
c.push(CASE("Express Newspapers v. Union of India, AIR 1958 SC 578","The Working Journalists Act is a valid labour law applying to the press as an industry; only its direct and inevitable effect on the freedom would invalidate it; the Wage Board's award set aside for considering the industry's overall capacity."));
c.push(CASE("ABP Pvt Ltd v. Union of India (2014) 3 SCC 327","The Majithia Wage Board's recommendations and the Government's 2011 notification upheld; the Act's Wage Board mechanism is constitutional; contempt proceedings in 2017 for non-implementation."));
c.push(CASE("Tata Press v. MTNL (1995) 5 SCC 139","Commercial speech is within Art 19(1)(a); advertising sustains the press; the Telegraph Rules did not bar a private buyers' guide."));
c.push(CASE("Common Cause v. Union of India (2015) 7 SCC 1 and review (2016)","Government advertising guidelines — public purpose, no personal glorification, limited photographs, a three-member content-regulation committee."));
c.push(CASE("Ushodaya Publications v. Government of A.P., AIR 1981 AP 109","The State's withdrawal of advertisements from Eenadu to punish its coverage was arbitrary and violated Art 14 and 19(1)(a); advertising is public money that cannot be used to coerce."));
c.push(CASE("K.M. Mathew v. State of Kerala (1992) 1 SCC 217 and K.M. Mathew v. K.A. Abraham (2002)","The PRB Act presumption of the editor's responsibility, and its limits."));
c.push(DIG("The Press Sewa portal (2024) registers periodicals online; the CBC's ad release is tracked through its portal; the Majithia arrears are enforced through State Labour Commissioners' online certificates; and digital news publishers must furnish details to the Ministry of I&B under Rule 18 of the 2021 IT Rules — the digital counterpart of newspaper registration, which the Bombay High Court has stayed in part."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Working journalist', 'Wage Board', 'Majithia award', 'Press Council of India — powers', 'Paid news', 'Press and Registration of Periodicals Act 2023', 'Press and the MRTP Act'. Part B: 'Explain the salient provisions of the Working Journalists Act 1955 relating to wages and conditions of service, and how the Wage Boards function' — Secs 2(f), 3-7, 9-12, 17, the Boards, Express Newspapers and ABP, the Labour Codes; or 'Discuss the relationship between advertising revenue and the freedom of the press, and the law relating to Government advertising' — Sakal, Tata Press, Common Cause, Ushodaya; or 'Examine the constitution, objects and powers of the Press Council of India'. Part C: a newspaper dismisses a sub-editor without notice — the ID Act applies (Sec 3), and Sec 4's notice; a newspaper refuses to pay the Majithia scales — recovery under Sec 17 and contempt (ABP); a State stops all advertisements to a paper critical of it — Ushodaya, arbitrary; a paper publishes a paid political profile as news — Press Council censure and Election Commission expense; a monopoly newspaper group buys the only other daily in a city — Competition Act Sec 5-6 if thresholds are met, otherwise no bar."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 05 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 05 — A Tale of Four Cities: K.A. Abbas and the Censorship of Films"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Films within freedom of speech and expression · Why pre-censorship is valid for films but not for the press · The Cinematograph Act 1952 — CBFC, certification, Sec 5B guidelines, the 2023 amendment · The film cases from Abbas to Padmaavat · Judicial review of certification decisions · The States and exhibition"));
c.push(STORY("Bombay, 1968. Khwaja Ahmad Abbas — screenwriter of Raj Kapoor's Awara and Shree 420, journalist, filmmaker — made a sixteen-minute documentary called 'A Tale of Four Cities' (Char Shaher Ek Kahani), contrasting the luxury of the rich in Calcutta, Bombay, Madras and Delhi with the squalor of the poor, and including a short sequence in Bombay's red-light district. He wanted a 'U' certificate so that the film could be shown to everyone. The Board of Film Censors offered a 'U' only if the red-light scenes were cut; the Central Government, on revision, gave a 'U' without cuts after the case was filed. Abbas pressed on with the constitutional question: was pre-censorship of films — submitting the film to a board before it could be shown — consistent with Art 19(1)(a)? Chief Justice Hidayatullah's answer has governed the subject since. Films are speech; censorship is a restriction; but pre-censorship of films is a reasonable restriction under Art 19(2) because a film is not a book: it combines sight and sound in a darkened hall, moves the emotions before the intellect, reaches the young and the impressionable, and can be shown to millions before any post-publication remedy could act. Yet the Court insisted on two conditions: the standards must be laid down with reasonable definiteness (the then-existing guidelines were too vague, and the Court read them down — the film must be judged as a whole, sex and violence are not per se objectionable, art and social purpose count), and the procedure must be fair, with a time limit and an appeal to a body independent of the executive — a promise the Government kept in the 1981 amendment by creating the Film Certification Appellate Tribunal, and broke in 2021 by abolishing it. The case is where the difference between visual and non-visual media becomes law."));
c.push(H3("Films and Art 19(1)(a)"));
c.push(P("A film is 'expression' — the film-maker's, the writer's, the actors' — and its exhibition is 'speech'; **K.A. Abbas** settled that films are within Art 19(1)(a), and the later cases treat the film-maker's freedom exactly as the press's, subject to the one difference of pre-certification. The right includes the right to make and exhibit the film (**S. Rangarajan v. P. Jagjivan Ram, 1989**), the right of the audience to see it (Rangarajan; Viacom 18 v. Union of India, 2018), and the right of a broadcaster to telecast it (Ramesh v. Union of India, 1988; LIC v. Manubhai D. Shah, 1992). The restrictions must fall under Art 19(2) and be reasonable; the CBFC is not a moral censor but a certifying authority applying statutory guidelines; and the courts have repeatedly held that the fear of a hostile audience (the 'heckler's veto') is no ground for banning a film — the State must protect the exhibition, not suppress the film (Rangarajan: 'freedom of expression cannot be held to ransom by an intolerant group of people'; Prakash Jha Productions v. Union of India, 2011 — Aarakshan; Viacom 18, 2018 — Padmaavat: States cannot ban a certified film)."));
c.push(H3("Why pre-censorship is valid for films but not for the press"));
c.push(P("**Abbas** gives the reasons: (1) the **impact** — 'the motion picture is able to stir up emotions more deeply than any other product of art'; its 'versatility, realism (often surrealism) and its coordination of the visual and aural senses' make it a medium of a different order; a film has 'a powerful influence on the mind' and 'can bring about a change in the mental attitude of the community'; (2) the **audience** — a mixed audience of all ages, in a public place, receiving the film as a whole, cannot select what to see as a reader selects what to read; the young are particularly affected; (3) the **irreversibility** — once a film is shown to lakhs, post-exhibition remedies are ineffective; a prosecution or a civil action cannot undo the harm; (4) the **history and comparative practice** — pre-censorship of films existed in every democracy (the United States, though the First Amendment forbids prior restraint, permits film licensing under Freedman v. Maryland's procedural safeguards); (5) the **Constitution** itself allows reasonable restrictions on all speech; pre-censorship is not per se unconstitutional if the standards are definite and the procedure fair; it fails for the press because the press cannot be subjected to a scrutiny before each issue without destroying its daily function (Brij Bhushan), and because the impact of print is less. The limits: (a) the standards must be definite (Abbas: 'the standards that we set for our censors must make a substantial allowance in favour of freedom'; the guidelines must not leave the censor a roving discretion); (b) the procedure must include a time limit and an appeal to a quasi-judicial body; (c) the censor must apply the reasonable-viewer, whole-film, social-purpose tests; (d) the Court reviews the certification decision (Episode's last section). **Rangarajan** adds the 'spark in a powder keg' test — the expression must be inseparably locked up with the action contemplated, like the equivalence of a spark in a powder keg — before public order can justify restriction; **Bobby Art International** (Bandit Queen) adds that a film must be judged by its message and impact as a whole, not by isolated scenes."));
c.push(H3("The Cinematograph Act 1952"));
c.push(SEC("Sec 2","Definitions — 'cinematograph' includes any apparatus for the representation of moving pictures; 'film'; 'certificate'; 'Board'; 'Tribunal' (omitted 2021); 'adult', 'child'; the 2023 amendment adds definitions for the age-based categories."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 3","The **Central Board of Film Certification** (CBFC — a Chairman and 12 to 25 members appointed by the Central Government, at Mumbai with regional offices, advisory panels of local members at each office) for the purpose of sanctioning films for public exhibition."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 4","Examination of films — the applicant applies for certification; the Board, after examining the film (by an examining committee of the CEO's officer and advisory panel members) may (i) sanction it for unrestricted public exhibition — **'U'**; (ii) sanction it for unrestricted exhibition with an endorsement of caution that parents should consider whether children under twelve (now age-graded) may see it — **'UA'**, subdivided by the 2023 amendment into **UA 7+, UA 13+ and UA 16+**; (iii) sanction it for exhibition restricted to adults — **'A'**; (iv) sanction it for exhibition restricted to members of any profession or class — **'S'**; (v) direct excisions or modifications before sanction; (vi) refuse to sanction. The 2023 Act also allows a separate certificate for television and other media for an 'A' film with modifications (Sec 4A)."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 5","Advisory panels; Sec 5A — the certificate, valid for ten years (perpetual after the 2023 amendment), and its publication in the Gazette; Sec 5C — appeal to the Tribunal (omitted 2021 — appeals now lie to the High Court under Art 226 after the Tribunals Reforms Act 2021 abolished the FCAT); Sec 5D — the Tribunal (omitted); Sec 5E-5F — suspension and revocation of certificates and review by the Central Government."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 5B — principles for guidance in certifying films","(1) A film shall not be certified for public exhibition if, in the opinion of the authority competent to grant the certificate, the film or any part of it is **against the interests of the sovereignty and integrity of India, the security of the State, friendly relations with foreign States, public order, decency or morality, or involves defamation or contempt of court, or is likely to incite the commission of any offence** — the Art 19(2) grounds copied into the statute (inserted by the 1981 amendment after Abbas); (2) the Central Government may issue **directions** setting out the principles which shall guide the certifying authority — the **Guidelines of 1991** (the current ones): the objectives (the medium of film remains responsible and sensitive to the values of society; artistic expression and creative freedom are not unduly curbed; certification is responsive to social change; the medium provides clean and healthy entertainment; the film is of aesthetic value and cinematically of a good standard) and the specific points (anti-social activities such as violence not glorified; the modus operandi of crime not depicted; scenes showing involvement of children in violence, abuse of children, cruelty to animals, needless cruelty, torture; scenes degrading women, sexual violence and rape shown only to the minimum necessary and if germane to the theme; scenes tending to encourage drug addiction and smoking; vulgarity, obscenity, depravity; dual-meaning words; visuals or words contemptuous of racial, religious or other groups; visuals or words promoting communal, obscurantist, anti-scientific and anti-national attitudes; the sovereignty and integrity of India; security of the State; friendly relations; public order; defamation and contempt; national symbols; the Board's duty to judge the film in its entirety from the point of view of its overall impact and in the light of contemporary standards, examining whether the film can be suitable for children if the guidelines are met). Rangarajan and Bobby Art International held that the Guidelines must be applied with the reasonable-viewer and whole-film approach."));
c.push(SEC("Sec 6","The Central Government's **revisional powers** — to call for the record of any proceeding of the Board and to declare a certified film uncertified or to change its certificate; **Union of India v. K.M. Shankarappa (2000)** struck down Sec 6(1) insofar as it allowed the Government to sit in appeal over the Tribunal's decision — the executive cannot review a quasi-judicial tribunal; the **2023 amendment omitted Sec 6(1)** as a consequence, leaving only the power to suspend exhibition in an emergency; Sec 6A-6B — information and advertisements of certified films; the 2023 Act inserted **Sec 6AA-6AB** (prohibition of unauthorised recording and exhibition — anti-piracy, punishable under Sec 7 with three years and a fine of up to five per cent of the audited gross production cost) and **Sec 7(1B)** (blocking of pirated content on the internet)."));
c.push(SEC("Secs 7-9","Penalties — exhibiting an uncertified film, or an 'A' film to a non-adult, or a film in altered form, or without the certificate's display: three years and ₹1 lakh (Sec 7); the 2023 Act adds the piracy offences; Sec 7A — powers of seizure by police; Sec 7B-7F — offences by companies; Sec 8 — rules (the Cinematograph (Certification) Rules 1983, replaced by the 2024 Rules — online applications, priority screening, accessibility features for the disabled, the 'exemption from re-certification' for age-graded versions); Sec 9 — the power to exempt (Government films, educational films)."));
c.push(SEC("Part III — Secs 10-17","Regulation of exhibition by means of cinematographs — licensing of cinema halls by the District Magistrate (Sec 10-11), applying to Union Territories; the States have their own **Cinemas (Regulation) Acts** (the Andhra Pradesh Cinemas (Regulation) Act 1955 — licensing of theatres, ticket prices, and the 2020-21 orders on ticket pricing; the Telangana Act after 2014), under **Entry 33 of List II** (theatres and dramatic performances; cinemas subject to the provisions of Entry 60 of List I — sanctioning of films for exhibition), so that the Centre certifies and the States license the halls and may impose local conditions but cannot ban a certified film on grounds of public order except by a proper order under the State Act or Sec 144 CrPC, which the courts review strictly (Viacom 18)."));
c.push(H3("The film cases"));
c.push(P("**K.A. Abbas v. Union of India (1970)** — pre-censorship valid; standards and procedure required. **Raj Kapoor v. State (Laxman) (1980)** — Satyam Shivam Sundaram; a CBFC certificate is a relevant (though not conclusive) consideration and a good defence under Sec 79 IPC (act done under a justification believed lawful) to a prosecution for obscenity under Sec 292; Justice Krishna Iyer: the certificate is the Board's expert view that the film is not obscene, and a magistrate should not lightly take cognizance against it. **S. Rangarajan v. P. Jagjivan Ram (1989)** — Ore Oru Gramathile, a Tamil film criticising caste-based reservation, given a 'U' and then revoked by the Madras High Court on the ground of threatened disorder; the Supreme Court restored it: the anticipated danger must not be remote or far-fetched but proximate and direct — the 'spark in a powder keg'; the State cannot plead its inability to maintain order; freedom of expression means the freedom to express views that a majority dislikes; the film was a legitimate criticism of Government policy. **Bobby Art International v. Om Pal Singh Hoon (1996)** — Bandit Queen; the Delhi High Court had quashed the 'A' certificate for scenes of nudity, rape and abuse; the Supreme Court restored it: the film must be judged as a whole by its message — the depiction of Phoolan Devi's degradation was needed to explain her revenge, and 'nakedness does not always arouse the baser instinct'; the Tribunal's decision (expert body) deserved deference. **Ramesh v. Union of India (1988)** — Tamas, a Doordarshan serial on Partition; the standard is the effect on a reasonable, strong-minded, firm and courageous man, not a weak and vacillating mind; the serial's purpose (to show the futility of communal violence) was legitimate. **Union of India v. K.M. Shankarappa (2000)** — the Government's revisional power over the Tribunal struck down. **Director General, Doordarshan v. Anand Patwardhan (2006)** — Father, Son and Holy War; DD must telecast an award-winning documentary certified 'A' that dealt with communal violence; the 'ordinary man of common sense and prudence' test. **Prakash Jha Productions v. Union of India (2011)** — Aarakshan; the U.P. Government's ban under the State Act set aside; a certified film cannot be suspended on the apprehension of law-and-order problems without a proper hearing and grounds. **Viacom 18 Media v. Union of India (2018)** — Padmaavat; four States' bans on a certified film were stayed; the States' power under the Cinemas Acts to suspend exhibition on public order grounds cannot be used to override the Board's certificate on the ground of the film's content; Chief Justice Misra: 'the film is certified... the exhibition cannot be prohibited on the ground of public order... the States must maintain law and order'. **Indibily Creative v. Government of West Bengal (2019)** — Bhobishyoter Bhoot; the State's informal pressure on theatres to withdraw a certified film was a violation of Art 19(1)(a) and the State was ordered to pay compensation; the Court condemned the 'shadow ban'. **Ajay Goswami (2007)** and **Aveek Sarkar (2014)** (Episode 03) on the community-standards test apply to films. **Ranjit Udeshi** and **Samaresh Bose** are cited for the obscenity standard. Documentary and telecast cases — LIC v. Manubhai D. Shah, Odyssey — are in Episode 06."));
c.push(H3("Judicial review of certification decisions"));
c.push(P("The courts review the CBFC's (and formerly the FCAT's) decisions under Art 226 and Art 32 on the usual grounds — error of law (applying the wrong test), irrelevant considerations (a hostile audience), failure to consider the film as a whole, disproportionate cuts, and breach of the Guidelines — while giving deference to the expert body's judgment on matters of taste and impact (Bobby Art). The 2021 abolition of the FCAT means that a producer aggrieved by a refusal or by cuts must go to the High Court, which lacks the Tribunal's expertise and speed; the Cinematograph (Amendment) Bill 2021's proposal to restore the Government's revisional power was dropped from the 2023 Act after protest. The courts have also examined: refusal of certification for documentaries (Anand Patwardhan's several cases), cuts on religious grounds (Vishwaroopam, 2013, Madras HC; the Tamil Nadu ban lifted), the 'S' certificate, the 'A' certificate's effect on television telecast (Sec 4A), and the Board's advisory panels' composition. Certification decisions are also reviewed for compliance with the Guidelines' 1991 procedure — the examining committee's reasons, the revising committee (Rule 24), and the show-cause before refusal."));
c.push(CASE("K.A. Abbas v. Union of India (1970) 2 SCC 780","Films are speech; pre-censorship of films is a reasonable restriction because of their impact; standards must be definite and the procedure fair with an appeal to an independent body."));
c.push(CASE("S. Rangarajan v. P. Jagjivan Ram (1989) 2 SCC 574","The threat of disorder is no ground to ban a certified film; the 'spark in a powder keg' proximity test; the State must protect the exhibition."));
c.push(CASE("Bobby Art International v. Om Pal Singh Hoon (1996) 4 SCC 1","Bandit Queen: a film is judged as a whole by its message; scenes of nudity and rape shown to convey the horror are not obscene; deference to the Tribunal."));
c.push(CASE("Raj Kapoor v. State (Laxman) (1980) 1 SCC 43","A CBFC certificate is a relevant defence under Sec 79 IPC to an obscenity prosecution; courts should not readily prosecute a certified film."));
c.push(CASE("Union of India v. K.M. Shankarappa (2001) 1 SCC 582","The Central Government's power to revise the Tribunal's decision is unconstitutional; the executive cannot sit in appeal over a quasi-judicial body."));
c.push(CASE("Viacom 18 Media v. Union of India (2018) 1 SCC 761","Padmaavat: State bans on a certified film stayed; the States' public-order power cannot override certification on the film's content."));
c.push(CASE("Indibily Creative v. Government of West Bengal (2020) 12 SCC 436","An informal 'shadow ban' by police pressure on theatres violates Art 19(1)(a); compensation awarded; the State must protect, not suppress."));
c.push(DIG("OTT films and series are not certified — the 2021 IT Rules make streaming platforms self-classify content (U, U/A 7+, 13+, 16+, A) with parental locks and a three-tier grievance mechanism (the publisher, a self-regulatory body headed by a retired judge, and the Ministry's Inter-Departmental Committee), which the Cinematograph (Amendment) Act 2023 borrowed for its age categories; the 2024 Certification Rules put applications online (e-Cinepramaan) and the Act's new Sec 7(1B) lets the Government order the blocking of pirated films — the Bombay and Delhi High Courts' 'dynamic injunctions' do the same for producers."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'K.A. Abbas case', 'Why pre-censorship for films', 'CBFC', 'Sec 5B', 'U, UA, A, S certificates', 'FCAT', 'Spark in a powder keg', 'Bandit Queen case', 'Padmaavat case'. Part B: 'Are films included in the freedom of speech and expression? Why is pre-censorship valid for films but not for the press? Discuss Abbas' — the five reasons and the conditions; or 'Describe the scheme of certification under the Cinematograph Act 1952 as amended in 2023 and the principles in Sec 5B and the Guidelines'; or 'Trace the judicial approach to film censorship from Abbas through Rangarajan, Bobby Art and Viacom 18'. Part C: a State bans a certified film because a caste group threatens violence — Rangarajan and Viacom 18, void; the Board refuses a 'U' for a documentary on slums with a red-light scene — Abbas, judge as a whole, social purpose; a magistrate takes cognizance of obscenity against a certified film — Raj Kapoor, the certificate is a Sec 79 defence; the Government reverses an appellate decision granting a certificate — Shankarappa, void; police 'advise' theatres not to screen a certified political satire — Indibily, compensation."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 06 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 06 — Who Owns the Airwaves? The Cricket Association of Bengal and the Government Monopoly in Broadcasting"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Radio and television as a Government monopoly — Sec 4 of the Telegraph Act, why a Government department · The case for an autonomous corporation — the Chanda Committee (1966), the Verghese Committee (1978), the Prasar Bharati Act 1990 · The effect of television on people · Government policy · Commercial advertisement on AIR and DD · Internal security of serials · Judicial review of Doordarshan decisions — the freedom to telecast · Cable television, private channels, FM and DTH"));
c.push(STORY("Calcutta, 1993. The Cricket Association of Bengal organised the Hero Cup, a six-nation one-day tournament for the Association's diamond jubilee, and sold the television rights to a foreign agency, Trans World International, which would produce the pictures and beam them by satellite to the world. Doordarshan wanted the rights for itself and, when refused, the Government invoked Sec 4 of the Indian Telegraph Act 1885: only the Government may establish or work a 'telegraph', which includes any apparatus transmitting signals by wireless; TWI's uplink from Eden Gardens needed a licence, and the licence was refused. The Calcutta High Court ordered the Government to let the signals go up; the Government appealed. The Supreme Court's three judges, in three separate opinions, said something the Constitution's authors had not imagined in 1950: the airwaves — the electromagnetic spectrum — are public property, not the Government's; the Government holds them as a trustee for the public; a monopoly of broadcasting in the Government's hands is not a restriction that Art 19(2) permits, because a Government monopoly is not a law in the interests of public order or security but the negation of the citizen's right to communicate; the right to impart and receive information through the airwaves belongs to the citizen — the organiser of a sporting event has the right to telecast it and the viewer to see it — subject to regulation by a public authority independent of Government control, to be created by Parliament. Justice Sawant, Justice Jeevan Reddy and Justice Mohan agreed on the principle and differed on the details; the direction to Parliament was never wholly obeyed — the Broadcasting Bill of 1997 lapsed — but the monopoly ended in practice within five years: private channels uplinked from India, private FM stations opened, and Doordarshan and All India Radio became the Prasar Bharati. The case is the constitutional foundation of every episode about broadcasting."));
c.push(H3("The Government monopoly — why a Government department?"));
c.push(P("Broadcasting in India began privately (the Radio Club of Bombay, 1923; the Indian Broadcasting Company, 1927, licensed under the Telegraph Act) and failed commercially; the Government took over the stations in 1930 as the Indian State Broadcasting Service, renamed **All India Radio** in 1936 under a Controller of Broadcasting in the Department of Industries and Labour; after 1947 AIR came under the **Ministry of Information and Broadcasting**, and **Doordarshan** (the television service, from 1959, separated from AIR in 1976) was a second department of the same Ministry. The **legal basis of the monopoly**: **Sec 4(1) of the Indian Telegraph Act 1885** — 'within India, the Central Government shall have the exclusive privilege of establishing, maintaining and working telegraphs', 'telegraph' being defined in Sec 3(1AA) to include any appliance for transmission or reception of signs, signals, writing, images and sounds by wire, visual or other electro-magnetic emissions, radio waves or Hertzian waves — so that broadcasting is a 'telegraph' and only the Government or its licensee may broadcast; the **Indian Wireless Telegraphy Act 1933** requires a licence to possess wireless apparatus (Sec 3) — the source of the old radio and television 'licence fee' abolished in 1985; **Entry 31 of List I** (posts and telegraphs; telephones, wireless, broadcasting and other like forms of communication) gives Parliament exclusive power. The **reasons** given for keeping broadcasting a Government department: (1) the scarcity of spectrum — a limited number of frequencies made a public monopoly the natural allocation (the argument of the BBC's charter and of the United States' 'public trustee' doctrine); (2) the cost — transmitters, studios and a national network needed public investment that no private party in 1947 could make; (3) national integration and development — broadcasting as an instrument of the State's policy in education, agriculture, family planning and unity (the Vidyalankar Committee's view; the SITE experiment of 1975-76; Krishi Darshan); (4) the fear of misuse by private, communal or foreign interests; (5) the wartime and security rationale — control of communication in emergencies. The **objections**: (1) a Government department under a Minister is the Government's voice — 'His Master's Voice', as AIR was called; the 1975 Emergency showed the danger; (2) the professional culture of a department (civil-service rules, transfers, audit) is unsuited to a creative medium; (3) the citizen's right to information (Art 19(1)(a)) is not served by a single, official source; (4) the monopoly is not a restriction under Art 19(2) but its negation (CAB); (5) technology (satellite, cable, the internet) made the monopoly unenforceable in any case."));
c.push(H3("Should there be an autonomous corporation? Chanda, Verghese and the Prasar Bharati Act"));
c.push(P("**The Chanda Committee (1964-66)** — the Committee on Broadcasting and Information Media chaired by A.K. Chanda, a former Comptroller and Auditor General — was the first to say that 'it is not possible in the Indian context for a creative medium like broadcasting to flourish under a regime of departmental rules and regulations'; it recommended that AIR and (a future) television be constituted as two **separate autonomous corporations** under Acts of Parliament, with boards of governors, financial and administrative autonomy, and the Government's control limited to policy directions in the national interest; it also recommended commercial advertising on AIR (accepted, 1967 — Vividh Bharati's commercial service) and the separation of television from radio (accepted, 1976). The Government (Indira Gandhi's) rejected autonomy as 'premature'. **The Verghese Committee (1977-78)** — the Working Group on Autonomy for Akashvani and Doordarshan chaired by B.G. Verghese, appointed by the Janata Government after the Emergency's abuse of broadcasting — recommended a **National Broadcast Trust ('Akash Bharati')** as a constitutionally protected, independent public corporation with a board of trustees selected by a broad-based panel, full programme autonomy, franchising of local stations, and public accountability through a Parliamentary committee and a Complaints Council; its Prasar Bharati Bill of 1979 lapsed with the Government. **The Joshi Committee (1985)** on software for Doordarshan ('An Indian Personality for Television') urged autonomy and social relevance. **The Prasar Bharati (Broadcasting Corporation of India) Act 1990** was passed by the National Front Government, notified only on 15 September 1997 (with the 1997 ordinance's amendments), and creates the **Prasar Bharati**: **Sec 3** — the Corporation, with AIR and DD transferred to it (Sec 3(3)); **Sec 3(4)** — the Board: a Chairman, an Executive Member (CEO), a Member (Finance), a Member (Personnel), six part-time members, the Directors General of AIR and DD ex officio, a Ministry representative and two representatives of employees; **Sec 4** — appointment by the President on the recommendation of a committee of the Vice-President, the Press Council chairman and a Presidential nominee; **Sec 12** — the **functions and powers**: to organise and conduct public broadcasting services to inform, educate and entertain the public, and to ensure a balanced development of broadcasting on radio and television, being guided by the objectives of upholding the unity and integrity of the country and the values of the Constitution, safeguarding the citizen's right to be informed freely, truthfully and objectively on all matters of public interest, paying special attention to the fields of education and the spread of literacy, agriculture, rural development, environment, health and family welfare and science and technology, providing adequate coverage to the diverse cultures, sports and games and youth affairs, promoting social justice, safeguarding the rights of the working classes, minorities and tribal communities, women and children, providing comprehensive broadcast coverage through the choice of appropriate technology, promoting research and development, expanding broadcasting facilities by establishing additional channels of transmission, and ensuring that broadcasting is conducted as a public service; **Sec 13** — the Parliamentary Committee (22 members) to oversee (never constituted); **Sec 14** — the **Broadcasting Council** (a President and ten members) to receive and consider complaints about programmes (constituted briefly); **Sec 23** — the Central Government's power to give **directions** in the interests of the sovereignty, integrity and security of India, friendly relations, public order, decency and morality — the residual State control; **Sec 24-26** — accounts, grants and the Corporation's funds; **Sec 32** — the recruitment and service conditions, with the pre-1997 employees remaining on deputation (the source of its two-decade staffing dispute, settled by the 2011 amendment and the Sam Pitroda Committee of 2014). In practice the Prasar Bharati has stayed dependent on the Ministry for funds (a grant-in-aid of about ₹3,000 crore a year) and for policy, and the debate about a genuinely independent public broadcaster (the Pitroda Committee's 2014 recommendations — a statutory funding formula, an independent board, a digital transition) continues; the 2023 Broadcasting Services (Regulation) Bill proposes a Broadcast Advisory Council for all broadcasters."));
c.push(H3("The effect of television on people; Government policy"));
c.push(P("Television's reach — from 41 transmitters in 1982 to the whole country after the INSAT expansion, from one channel to hundreds, from 8 million sets in 1985 to 210 million television homes in 2024 — made it the dominant medium of information and entertainment, and its **effects** are the substance of the 'impact on people's minds' topic: the homogenising and integrating effect of national programmes (the Ramayan and Mahabharat serials of 1987-90, the Hum Log soap of 1984 modelled on Mexican pro-development telenovelas), the commercialisation of the medium and its content after the 1982 colour transition and the 1985 sponsored programme scheme, the concern about consumerism, violence, the 'cultural invasion' of satellite channels (the Zee, Star and MTV debates of the 1990s), the effects on children (the Cable Advertising Code's ban on ads that exploit children's credulity; the ASCI's guidelines), on women (the Indecent Representation Act), on elections (the Election Commission's rules on paid political advertising and exit polls, Sec 126A of the Representation of the People Act 1951), on communal harmony (the 1992-93 riot coverage; the Programme Code's ban on content that offends religious sentiments or promotes communal disharmony), and the 'trial by media' debate (Episode 11). **Government policy** moved through five phases: (1) 1959-76 — development broadcasting under Vikram Sarabhai's vision (SITE); (2) 1976-91 — Doordarshan's expansion, commercialisation and use as a Government instrument (the Joshi Committee's criticism); (3) 1991-97 — the satellite invasion, the Cable Act 1995, the CAB judgment, and the 1997 Prasar Bharati notification; (4) 1997-2010 — liberalisation by executive guidelines: uplinking (1999-2000, revised 2005, 2011), downlinking (2005), DTH (2003), FM Phase I (2000) and Phase II (2005), community radio (2002 educational, 2006 NGOs), the TRAI as broadcast regulator (2004), the Conditional Access System (2003) and digitisation (2011 amendment to the Cable Act, four phases to 2017); (5) 2010 onward — the shift to digital, the 2021 IT Rules for OTT, the 2022 uplinking and downlinking guidelines (a channel must broadcast thirty minutes of public-service content daily), the 2023 Broadcasting Bill (withdrawn in 2024 for redrafting), and the Telecommunications Act 2023 replacing the Telegraph Act. The **National Broadcasting Policy** promised since the 1990s has not been adopted; the policy remains a set of guidelines and licence conditions."));
c.push(H3("Commercial advertisement on AIR and Doordarshan"));
c.push(P("Advertising on AIR began on 1 November 1967 (Vividh Bharati's commercial service, on Chanda's recommendation) and on Doordarshan on 1 January 1976; sponsored programmes from 1980 (Hum Log, 1984, was the first sponsored serial). The **Commercial Code** — AIR's Code for Commercial Advertising (1967, revised), Doordarshan's Commercial Code (1976, revised 1986, 1997 and after) — bars advertisements of cigarettes, liquor, tobacco, of political or religious nature, of fortune tellers, of anything derogatory to women or offensive to religion, claims that mislead, and content contrary to the Advertising Code under the Cable Act; limits advertising time (the TRAI's 2012 twelve-minutes-an-hour rule for private channels, stayed for several years; Prasar Bharati sets its own); and requires sponsored programmes to be identified. Advertising is the Prasar Bharati's second source of funds after the grant (about ₹1,000 crore a year, declining as viewers move to private channels and streaming). The legal issues: whether the Prasar Bharati can refuse an advertisement (it is 'State', so its refusal must be non-arbitrary — Tata Press's principle applied; a political party's advertisement may be refused under the Code); whether Doordarshan's monopoly on terrestrial advertising was an abuse (never litigated; the 1990s cases were about programme access, not advertising); and whether Government advertising through Prasar Bharati is subject to the Common Cause guidelines (it is)."));
c.push(H3("'Internal security' of serials and the pre-telecast scrutiny"));
c.push(P("Doordarshan's programmes were and are subject to **internal scrutiny** before telecast — a preview committee under the Director General applying the Broadcasting Code (the 1969 code adopted at the Fourth Asian Broadcasting Conference, incorporated into AIR's and DD's rules: no criticism of friendly countries, no attack on religion or community, nothing obscene or defamatory, no incitement to violence, nothing against the maintenance of law and order, nothing that amounts to contempt of court, nothing aspersing the integrity of the President, the Governors and the judiciary, nothing that affects the integrity of the nation, no criticism by name of any person) — and to the Cinematograph Act's certification for feature films (Sec 4A now allows a television version). The **'internal security' phrase in the syllabus** refers to this pre-telecast control: DD may refuse a serial or a documentary on the ground that it endangers security, public order or communal harmony, and the question is whether that refusal is reviewable. The cases show that it is: the producer's freedom to telecast is an Art 19(1)(a) right against a State broadcaster, and DD's refusal must be on Art 19(2) grounds and reasonable. **Ramesh v. Union of India (1988)** — Tamas: DD's decision to telecast the serial on Partition was challenged as likely to incite communal violence; the Court (applying the reasonable, strong-minded viewer standard) upheld the telecast — the serial as a whole promoted communal harmony by showing the horror of violence. **Odyssey Communications v. Lokvidayan Sanghatana (1988)** — Honi Anhoni: a public interest petition sought to stop a serial on the supernatural as promoting superstition; the Supreme Court set aside the Bombay High Court's injunction — the right of a citizen to exhibit films on Doordarshan, subject to DD's terms, is part of Art 19(1)(a), and a serial cannot be stopped on the ground that it spreads superstition unless it falls within Art 19(2). **LIC v. Manubhai D. Shah (1992)** — two cases decided together: LIC refused to publish a rejoinder to an article in its magazine Yogakshema (held: LIC is 'State' and must publish the reply — fairness under Art 14 and 19(1)(a)); and Doordarshan refused to telecast **'Beyond Genocide'**, an award-winning documentary on the Bhopal gas disaster, on the ground that it was one-sided and that the claims were sub judice (held: DD, a State monopoly, cannot refuse to telecast a documentary that had won the National Award unless its content falls within Art 19(2); the sub judice plea was irrelevant; the film-maker's freedom of expression includes the right to convey his perception through the State broadcaster on reasonable terms). **Anand Patwardhan's cases** — Bombay: Our City (1986), In Memory of Friends (1989), Ram Ke Naam (1992, the Bombay High Court ordered DD to telecast the film on the Ayodhya movement), Father, Son and Holy War (the Supreme Court, 2006, ordered the telecast — the Board's 'A' certificate did not justify DD's refusal; the test is the ordinary person of common sense). **Secretary, I&B v. CAB (1995)** — the freedom to telecast a sporting event. These cases establish the **judicial review of Doordarshan's decisions**: DD is 'State' under Art 12; its refusal to telecast is an administrative decision reviewable for Art 14 fairness and Art 19(2) compliance; it cannot act as a censor beyond the statutory grounds; but it retains programming discretion on quality, scheduling and terms (Prasar Bharati v. Sahara TV Network, 2005 — the 'must share' rule for national events, later codified in the Sports Broadcasting Signals (Mandatory Sharing with Prasar Bharati) Act 2007 — Union of India v. BCCI, 2017: the shared feed is for DD's own terrestrial and DTH networks only, not for re-transmission by cable operators)."));
c.push(H3("Cable, satellite, FM and DTH — the end of the monopoly in practice"));
c.push(P("The **Cable Television Networks (Regulation) Act 1995** (after the 1994 ordinance) legitimised the cable operators who had sprung up in 1991-92 to distribute satellite channels: **Sec 3-4** — registration of cable operators with the Head Post Office (now the registering authority); **Sec 4A** — digital addressable systems (2011 amendment); **Sec 5** — programmes must conform to the **Programme Code** (Rule 6 of the Cable Television Network Rules 1994 — no programme that offends good taste or decency, criticises friendly countries, attacks religions or communities or contains visuals or words contemptuous of religious groups or promoting communal attitudes, contains anything obscene, defamatory, deliberate, false and suggestive innuendos and half-truths, is likely to encourage or incite violence or contains anything against the maintenance of law and order or which promotes anti-national attitudes, contains anything amounting to contempt of court, casts aspersions against the integrity of the President and judiciary, affects the integrity of the nation, criticises, maligns or slanders any individual or group, encourages superstition or blind belief, denigrates women, denigrates children, contains visuals or words that reflect a slandering, ironical and snobbish attitude in the portrayal of certain ethnic, linguistic and regional groups, contravenes the Cinematograph Act, is unsuitable for unrestricted public exhibition, and — since 2021 — the self-regulatory tiers); **Sec 6** — the **Advertising Code** (Rule 7); **Sec 8** — the must-carry obligation for DD's channels; **Sec 9** — Indian-made equipment; **Sec 10** — no interference with telecommunication; **Sec 11-13** — seizure and confiscation; **Sec 16-18** — penalties (two years, ₹1,000, five years for a second offence); **Sec 19** — the authorised officer's power to prohibit transmission of a programme not in conformity with the Codes or likely to promote disharmony; **Sec 20** — the Central Government's power to prohibit operation of a cable network in the public interest, the sovereignty and integrity of India, security, friendly relations or public order, and to regulate or prohibit the transmission of any channel or programme. The **satellite channels** are licensed by the Ministry's Uplinking and Downlinking Guidelines (2022 consolidation): an Indian company with a net worth threshold, 26 per cent foreign cap for news, security clearance, compliance with the Programme and Advertising Codes, and the Inter-Ministerial Committee's power to issue advisories and to order a channel off air (the 2021 IT Rules' three-tier system now runs in parallel for news channels' digital feeds). **FM radio**: Phase I (2000) licence fees, Phase II (2005) one-time entry fee and four per cent revenue share, Phase III (2011-15) e-auctions and networking, with no private news (relay of AIR bulletins allowed since 2017); **community radio** (2006 policy) for NGOs, universities and KVKs, with local content and limited advertising; **DTH** (2003 guidelines; six operators; the licence fee at eight per cent of adjusted gross revenue after 2020); **HITS** and **IPTV**; and the **TRAI Act 1997**, under which broadcasting and cable were notified as 'telecommunication services' in 2004 so that the TRAI regulates tariffs (the 2017 new tariff order — à la carte pricing), interconnection and quality of service, and the TDSAT decides disputes. The **Telecommunications Act 2023** replaces the Telegraph Act 1885 and the Wireless Telegraphy Act 1933 (in phases from 2024): Sec 3 — authorisation by the Central Government for telecommunication services, networks and radio equipment; Sec 4 — spectrum assignment by auction, with the First Schedule's administrative assignment for broadcasting (satellite) and Government uses; Sec 20 — the Government's powers of interception and suspension in a public emergency or for public safety (the Anuradha Bhasin safeguards in the 2024 Rules); the monopoly language of Sec 4 of the 1885 Act is gone, replaced by authorisation."));
c.push(CASE("Secretary, Ministry of I&B v. Cricket Association of Bengal (1995) 2 SCC 161","Airwaves are public property; a Government monopoly of broadcasting violates Art 19(1)(a); the right to telecast and to receive; regulation by an independent public authority."));
c.push(CASE("Odyssey Communications v. Lokvidayan Sanghatana (1988) 3 SCC 410","The right to exhibit a serial on Doordarshan is part of Art 19(1)(a); a serial cannot be stopped for 'spreading superstition' unless it falls within Art 19(2)."));
c.push(CASE("Ramesh v. Union of India (1988) 1 SCC 668","Tamas: the reasonable, strong-minded viewer test; a serial showing the horror of communal violence promotes harmony; telecast permitted."));
c.push(CASE("LIC v. Manubhai D. Shah (1992) 3 SCC 637","Doordarshan cannot refuse to telecast an award-winning documentary on Bhopal on the ground that it is one-sided; DD is State; the freedom to convey one's perception through the State broadcaster; LIC must publish a rejoinder."));
c.push(CASE("Director General, Doordarshan v. Anand Patwardhan (2006) 8 SCC 433","DD must telecast a certified award-winning documentary; the ordinary-person test; the 'A' certificate is not a ground for refusal."));
c.push(CASE("Union of India v. Board of Control for Cricket in India (2017) 2 SCC 333","Under the Sports Broadcasting Signals Act 2007, the feed shared with Prasar Bharati may be carried on DD's own networks only, not re-transmitted by private cable operators under the Cable Act's must-carry rule."));
c.push(DIG("Prasar Bharati's DD Free Dish (the free DTH platform with 45 million homes), the NewsOnAir app and DD's YouTube channels are the public broadcaster's digital face; the TRAI's 2023 recommendations and the 2024 draft Broadcasting Bill would bring OTT and digital news under a common regulatory council; the Telecommunications Act's spectrum provisions govern 5G broadcasting (D2M — direct-to-mobile, being piloted); and the Supreme Court's CAB principle — spectrum as a public trust — was applied in the 2G case (Centre for Public Interest Litigation v. Union of India, 2012) to require auctions."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Chanda Committee', 'Verghese Committee', 'Prasar Bharati', 'Airwaves as public property', 'Sec 4 Telegraph Act', 'Programme Code', 'Freedom to telecast', 'Must-share rule'. Part B: 'Why was broadcasting a Government department in India? Should it be an autonomous corporation? Discuss with reference to the Chanda and Verghese Committees and the Prasar Bharati Act' — the reasons and objections, the two committees, Secs 3, 12, 14, 23; or 'Discuss the judicial review of Doordarshan's decisions on the freedom to telecast' — Odyssey, Ramesh, Manubhai Shah, Patwardhan, CAB; or 'Describe the legal framework for cable and satellite television in India'. Part C: DD refuses to telecast a documentary critical of a Government scheme as 'one-sided' — Manubhai Shah, must telecast unless Art 19(2); a State asks cable operators to drop a channel for criticising the Chief Minister — Sec 19-20 of the Cable Act require the Codes' grounds, otherwise void; a private FM station broadcasts its own news bulletin — breach of licence conditions (policy, not statute); an event organiser sells telecast rights to a foreign producer and the Government refuses the uplink — CAB, the refusal must be justified by a law under Art 19(2) and a regulatory framework."));
c.push(PB());

module.exports=c;
